ry":"Students trace the causal chain from a specific data-quality problem to the specific output mistake it causes, and separate the quality of a dataset from its size.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU"],"objectives":["Explain, with an example, how higher- or lower-quality data changes an AI output.","Predict the effect on the output when the data quality is degraded or improved.","Distinguish the quality of a dataset from the quantity of data in it."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Data Quality and AI Outputs","body":["Imagine teaching a younger cousin to recognise dates using a photo album. If half the photos are blurry and one photo is actually a stone, incorrectly labelled as a date, what will your cousin get wrong? An AI learns the same way you would teach - from the examples it is shown. Today you become a data detective who can predict an AI's mistakes just by studying its data."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can explain, using a clear example, how the quality of data changes what an AI produces.","I can predict whether an output will get better or worse when the data is made cleaner or noisier, and say why.","I can tell the difference between the amount of data and the quality of data."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"data quality","definition":"How accurate, complete, consistent, and representative a dataset is."},{"term":"label","definition":"The name attached to an example that tells the model what it is."},{"term":"noise","definition":"Irrelevant or inconsistent detail in data that confuses the pattern."},{"term":"representative","definition":"The data covers the real range of cases the model will meet."},{"term":"causal chain","definition":"A step-by-step link from a cause to its effect."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"The model learns from its examples","body":["An AI model is not filled with rules that a person typed. Instead it studies a dataset and adjusts itself to match the patterns 