oise, then gaps, then error worst, because wrong labels teach an actively false pattern while noise only adds confusion. Accept other orders if the justification cites output evidence; if a ranking has no evidence, prompt 'point to the output change that proves it'.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Investigation log: record each dataset version, its output change, its data flaw, and its dimension scores.","stem":"Version ___ | Output change vs baseline ___ | Data flaw ___ | Accuracy/Complete/Consistent score ___","lookFor":"Specific, accurate output changes recorded for every version, each linked to its data flaw, with the three dimensions scored. Not yet: vague impressions with no link to a data flaw.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: which single data flaw would you fix first to improve the output the most, and why?","lookFor":"Names one flaw from their own log and justifies it from the output evidence they gathered, rather than from opinion or a general statement about data quality.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w1-lab-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): invent a fifth dataset that combines two flaws. Predict where it lands on the ranking line and justify your prediction with the evidence you gathered.","lookFor":"Weighs two flaws against each other and places the combined dataset using evidence from the four-way comparison rather than a hunch.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"}]}},"g6-bridging-w2-core":{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core","number":3,"week":2,"track":"bridging","type":"core","title":"Refining Prompts for Better Outputs","summary":"Students learn a refinement loop - set the goal, judge the output, name one weakness, make a single targeted change, and re-judge - so they can say exactly why a prompt change improved the result.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["SD"],"objectives":["Improve a weak prompt so the output better meets the goal.","Explain which change improved the output and why it worked."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["You ask an AI to 'write about Al Ain' and get a dull, general paragraph. Was the AI wrong - or was the prompt?","Today you become a prompt engineer who sets a clear goal, judges the output against it, and makes one smart change at a time. Best of all, you will be able to say exactly why your change worked."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can turn a weak prompt into a stronger one that better meets the goal.","I can name the exact change I made and explain why it improved the output.","I can judge an output against the goal instead of just accepting it."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"prompt","definition":"The instruction and context you give an AI to guide its output."},{"term":"goal","definition":"What you want the output to achieve, stated clearly."},{"term":"constraint","definition":"A limit you set, such as length, tone, or format."},{"term":"ambiguous","definition":"Unclear or open to more than one meaning."},{"term":"refinement loop","definition":"Goal, judge, name the weakness, change, re-judge - repeated."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Judge before you change","body":["You cannot improve an output until you judge it honestly against the [[goal]]. Ask: what is missing, and what is off-target?","Common weaknesses in a prompt are a missing audience, missing format, missing [[constraint]], or an [[ambiguous]] task. Naming the weakness tells you exactly what to fix."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Change one thing, and know why","body":["Strong refiners change **one thing at a time** and predict why it should help before they test it. That way, when the output improves, they know which change did it.","Specificity comes from the task, the audience, and the format - never from personal or private information. A precise [[prompt]] is not a personal one."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will take a weak prompt with a clear goal, name one weakness, write a targeted v2, predict its effect, and check against the revealed output."],"items":["Read the weak prompt and its goal.","Judge the v1 output and name one weakness.","Write a v2 that targets only that weakness.","Predict how the output should change and why.","Compare with the revealed v2 output and log it."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Why is changing one thing at a time smarter than changing many?","How can you make a prompt specific without adding any private information?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the one change that most improved your output and why it worked.","Describe a time a clearer instruction would have helped you get a better result in real life."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 2 - Core - Worksheet | Refining Prompts for Better Outputs","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-q1","type":"shortText","prompt":"For the weak prompt given, name one weakness and write a v2 that targets only that weakness.","stem":"The weakness is ___.\nMy v2 prompt is ___.","lookFor":"Names a weakness that genuinely matches the output problem (ambiguous task, or missing audience, format, or constraint), and a v2 that directly targets that one named weakness rather than bundling several edits or simply lengthening the prompt.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-q2","type":"shortText","prompt":"After the reveal, explain the causal reason your change improved the output.","stem":"My change was ___.\nIt improved the output because ___.","lookFor":"A causal explanation linking the specific change to the improvement (e.g. naming the audience and length removed the model's need to guess, so the output became focused and age-appropriate). Reward causal reasoning, not 'it is better'.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-q3","type":"shortText","prompt":"Record your v1, the weakness, your v2 change, the predicted effect, and the observed effect.","stem":"v1 prompt | Weakness named | v2 change | Predicted vs observed effect","lookFor":"A usable log in which one change is recorded per row with a stated reason, so the improvement can be attributed to a cause; predicted and observed effects are both recorded and compared, not just asserted.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-q4","type":"shortText","prompt":"Explain in one or two sentences which prompt change improved your output and the reason it worked.","stem":"","lookFor":"Names one specific change and gives a clear causal reason for the improvement. Partial: a general reason with a weak causal link. Not yet: states it is better with no reason.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-core-q5","type":"shortText","prompt":"Write a v3 that improves the output on a second goal (for example, making it both clearer and more accurate) without losing the first improvement.","stem":"","lookFor":"A v3 that adds a gain on a second dimension while preserving the first gain, with reasoning that shows awareness that the two goals must both be met rather than traded away.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]}]}},"g6-bridging-w2-lab":{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab","number":4,"week":2,"track":"bridging","type":"lab","title":"Prompt Refinement Workshop (Lab)","summary":"Teams iterate a weak prompt across several versions with a one-change-per-version log, then refine for clarity and accuracy together and justify a best-balanced version from their trade-off evidence.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["SD"],"objectives":["Iterate a prompt across several versions to raise output quality, logging each change and its effect.","Refine for two goals at once (clarity and accuracy) and discuss the trade-off."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["In the Core lesson you made one smart change to a prompt. Today you run a full workshop: version 1, 2, 3, maybe 4 - each a single change you can defend.","Then comes the real challenge of a designer: chasing two goals at once, clarity and accuracy, and discovering that helping one can cost the other. Your log will prove exactly what worked."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can iterate a prompt across several versions and log each change and its effect.","I can refine for two goals at once and explain the trade-off between them.","I can use my log as evidence for which change helped the most."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"iterate","definition":"To improve something step by step across several versions."},{"term":"trade-off","definition":"When improving one goal makes another goal worse."},{"term":"balance","definition":"The best compromise between competing goals for a purpose."},{"term":"accuracy","definition":"How correct and on-topic the output is."},{"term":"clarity","definition":"How easy the output is to understand."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Iterate and keep the receipts","body":["A designer does not stop at the first improvement. You [[iterate]] - version by version - changing one thing each time and logging its effect.","The log is your **evidence**. When someone asks which change helped most, you point to the row that proves it, because you changed only one thing that version."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Two goals, one trade-off","body":["Now aim for two goals at once: [[clarity]] and [[accuracy]]. Sometimes a change that makes the output clearer quietly drops an accurate detail.","That is a [[trade-off]]. Your job is not to pretend it away but to choose the version that best balances both goals for your purpose - and to say why."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Your team iterates a weak prompt across several versions, logging each change and effect, then balances clarity and accuracy and chooses the best version with evidence."],"items":["Set the goal and judge the v1 output.","Make v2 with one change; log the reason and revealed effect.","Iterate to v3 and v4, one change each, logging cause and effect.","Add accuracy as a second goal alongside clarity.","Find and record a trade-off where one goal cost the other.","Choose the best-balanced version and justify it from your log."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which single change gave the biggest quality jump, and how does your log prove it?","Where did clarity and accuracy pull against each other, and how did you decide?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the best-balanced version your team chose and one sentence on why.","Explain what balancing two goals taught you about making design decisions."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 2 - Lab - Worksheet | Prompt Refinement Workshop (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-q1","type":"shortText","prompt":"For each version, write the one change you made, your reason, and the effect on the output.","stem":"Version ___ change: ___.\nReason: ___.\nEffect: ___.","lookFor":"One single change per version with a clear cause-and-effect entry (e.g. v2 added the audience 'Grade 3'; reason: remove guessing; effect: output became simpler and on-level). Watch for entries that bundle several edits or that only lengthen the prompt with no reason.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-q2","type":"shortText","prompt":"Describe one point where improving clarity hurt accuracy (or the reverse), then choose your best-balanced version and justify it.","stem":"Improving ___ hurt ___ when I ___.\nMy best-balanced version is ___ because ___.","lookFor":"A specific place where one goal cost the other (e.g. cutting the notice to two sentences for clarity dropped the accurate event time), plus a best-balanced version justified from the log against the purpose. Accept any version if justified; the last version is not automatically best.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-q3","type":"shortText","prompt":"Record each version's change, reason, clarity effect, and accuracy effect.","stem":"Version | Single change | Reason | Effect on clarity / accuracy","lookFor":"Several versions logged, each with one change and a clear cause-effect entry across both goals. Quality of reasoning per row matters more than the number of rows.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-q4","type":"shortText","prompt":"Which version was your best balance of clarity and accuracy, and what did choosing it cost you?","stem":"","lookFor":"Names a version as the best balance and states the cost explicitly - what was given up on the other goal - showing the student expects trade-offs rather than claiming every goal improved at no cost.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w2-lab-q5","type":"shortText","prompt":"Add a third goal, tone, and describe in a few lines how balancing three goals is harder than balancing two, using your log.","stem":"","lookFor":"Adds tone as a third goal and explains, with reference to log evidence, why more goals means more competing costs and a harder choice of best balance.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"}]}]}},"g6-bridging-w3-core":{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core","number":5,"week":3,"track":"bridging","type":"core","title":"How Data Shapes a Solution's Outcome","summary":"Students trace how the examples a fruit-ripeness sorter learned from decide the answer it gives, then predict how changing that data would move the outcome.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CE"],"objectives":["Explain how the data used changes the outcome of a given solution.","Predict how a change in the data would change the solution's result."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Two apps can share the exact same code and still disagree about the same photo. The secret is not in the code it is in what each app was shown. Today you become a data detective, tracing how the examples an app learns from decide the answer it gives."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can explain, in my own words, how the data fed into a solution shapes the outcome it produces.","I can predict what would happen to the outcome if the data were changed, and give a reason.","I can point to a specific part of the data that most affects the result."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"solution","definition":"The whole system that solves a problem - the data plus the model built from it."},{"term":"data","definition":"The examples a solution learns from."},{"term":"outcome","definition":"The answer or result the solution gives."},{"term":"pattern","definition":"The regularity a model finds across many examples."},{"term":"balance","definition":"Whether the data has roughly fair amounts of each category."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"A solution is data plus a model","body":["When people say an app 'decides', they often picture a programmer writing every rule by hand. Machine-learning solutions do not work that way. They build a **model** by studying [[data]] - lots of examples - and capturing the [[pattern]]s inside.","Because the pattern comes from the examples, the examples quietly control the answer. Feed the model different data and it learns a different pattern, so it gives a different [[outcome]], even with the very same code."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Amount and balance both matter","body":["Data influences an outcome in two ways. First, the *amount*: if the model saw 100 sunny photos and only 3 rainy ones, it treats sunny as normal and stumbles on rain.","Second, the [[balance]]: adding more of what the model already has plenty of changes little, but adding examples of a rare or missing category can move the outcome a lot. A data detective looks for the gap."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Your teacher will run a fruit-ripeness sorter on the board. Your job is not to run it - it is to trace why it answers the way it does, using the data list on your card."],"items":["Read the data list on your card and say what the sorter mostly saw.","Watch one fruit go through and note the outcome.","Draw an arrow from the data batch you think caused that outcome.","Write: 'The outcome is ___ because the data mostly showed ___.'","Check your reason against a second fruit - does the story still hold?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which data batch had the biggest effect on the outcome, and how do you know?","If you could add only one batch of new data, which would you add to make the sorter fairer?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write one sentence explaining how data shaped today's outcome.","Note one thing you would check before trusting a solution's answer."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 3 - Core - Worksheet | How Data Shapes a Solution's Outcome","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-g6-bw3c-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Trace the data: use the worked solution card. Draw an arrow from the data batch to the outcome, then explain the link in one sentence.","stem":"The outcome is ___\nbecause the data mostly showed ___","lookFor":"A named data feature causing the outcome - for example that the training data was mostly ripe brown dates, so the sorter treats brown as the normal ripe look - rather than a general 'the data did it'.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-g6-bw3c-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"What if the data changed? Predict how the outcome changes if we add 50 green unripe date photos. Point to the exact data that causes your predicted change.","stem":"I predict the outcome will ___\nbecause the added data ___","lookFor":"Reasoning that links the added category to the shifted outcome - more green dates labelled 'unripe' correctly because the sorter now has examples of a category it was missing. A plausible shift with only vague justification is partial.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-g6-bw3c-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Data change log: for each data change, record what changed and how the outcome moved. Use the columns Data change | Predicted outcome | Which data caused it | Confidence (high/med/low).","lookFor":"Each row attributes the predicted movement to a specific data batch rather than to luck or to the code, and the confidence rating is used honestly on the rows where the data link is weaker.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"process"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-g6-bw3c-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: explain in two sentences how the data shaped the sorter's outcome, and predict one data change that would flip it.","lookFor":"A clear path traced from specific data to the outcome, plus a prediction of the shift justified by pointing to the exact data changed. Attributing the outcome to luck, the code or something being broken is not yet there.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-core-g6-bw3c-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): design the smallest possible data change that would flip the sorter from 'ripe' to 'unripe' on a brown date, and explain why so small a change can have such a big effect.","lookFor":"The gap-filling idea: a small batch that supplies a category the sorter barely had moves the outcome more than adding to a category it already has plenty of. Look for the smallest change named precisely, not just 'add more data'.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"}]}},"g6-bridging-w3-lab":{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-lab","number":6,"week":3,"track":"bridging","type":"lab","title":"Trace-the-Data Investigation (Lab)","summary":"Students run three controlled single data swaps on the ripeness sorter, log the before-and-after outcomes, and use that evidence to identify and verify the most influential change.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CE"],"objectives":["Change the data in a worked solution and observe and record the effect on the outcome.","Identify which single data change most alters the outcome, and explain why."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["In the Core lesson you predicted how a data change would move the outcome. A prediction is only a guess until you test it. Today you run a fair test: change one thing, watch the outcome, and let the evidence tell you which data matters most."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can run a controlled data change and record the outcome before and after.","I can compare data changes and identify which one moved the outcome the most.","I can explain, using my recorded evidence, why that change had the biggest effect."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"controlled change","definition":"Changing one thing at a time so you know what caused the result."},{"term":"before/after","definition":"The outcome measured just before and just after a single change."},{"term":"shift","definition":"How far the outcome moved because of a change."},{"term":"influential","definition":"Having a large effect on the outcome."},{"term":"verify","definition":"To re-test and confirm an effect is real, not a one-off."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Change one thing at a time","body":["To find out how much a data batch matters, you must isolate it. Measure the outcome **before**, change only that one batch, and measure again. The difference is the influence of that batch.","If you change two batches at once and the outcome moves, you cannot tell which batch did it. A fair test changes one variable at a time - the same rule scientists use in any experiment."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Evidence beats opinion","body":["Your log is your evidence. Without a written before-and-after, you are relying on memory, and memory blurs across many swaps.","Once you have run several swaps, the most [[influential]] batch is the one with the biggest [[shift]]. [[Verify]] it by re-running on a fresh fruit - if the effect repeats, you have proof, not a coincidence."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Work in threes with roles: swapper, recorder, checker. You will run three single data swaps, log each outcome, and find the most influential change."],"items":["Write your Core prediction at the top of the log.","Record the BEFORE outcome for your test fruit.","Swap in one data batch and record the AFTER outcome.","Note the shift: none, a little, or a lot.","Repeat for two more single swaps.","Rank the swaps and star the most influential.","Verify the winner on a fresh fruit and write your conclusion."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Did the evidence match your prediction? Where did it surprise you?","Why might a small batch beat a large one for influence?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write your evidence-based conclusion about the most influential data change.","Note one thing a fair test taught you that a guess could not."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 3 - Lab - Worksheet | Trace-the-Data Investigation (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-lab-g6-bw3l-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Run three single swaps: for each swap, record the before outcome, the after outcome, and the size of the shift. Change only one batch at a time.","stem":"Before: ___\nAfter: ___\nShift: ___","lookFor":"One batch changed at a time with a clean before value recorded before the swap, not filled in afterwards. Changing several batches at once means the outcome cannot be attributed and the swap should be redone.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"process"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-lab-g6-bw3l-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Name and verify the winner: state which swap was most influential, justify it from your log, and describe how you verified it on a fresh fruit.","stem":"The most influential swap was ___\nMy log shows ___\nI verified it by ___","lookFor":"The winner is the batch that filled the biggest data gap - typically the green-date batch - justified from the logged before/after shifts rather than opinion, and confirmed by a re-test on a fresh fruit that shows the effect repeats.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-lab-g6-bw3l-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Investigation log: log every single swap and its effect so you can rank them fairly. Use the columns Swap (one batch) | Before outcome | After outcome | Shift size | Rank.","lookFor":"Every row records one batch only, with both before and after outcomes filled in and a shift size (none / a little / a lot). A missing before value or a muddled swap makes the ranking unreliable.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"process"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-lab-g6-bw3l-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: state the most influential data change you found and give the recorded evidence that proves it.","lookFor":"Evidence, not opinion: the named swap is backed by the before/after shift written in the log, and ideally by the repeat result from the verification run.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w3-lab-g6-bw3l-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): design a data swap you predict will produce zero outcome change, then test it. Explain why some data has no influence on this solution.","lookFor":"A swap that only adds more of what the sorter already has plenty of - for example another brown-date batch - with the explanation that it repeats a pattern the model already learned, so it fills no gap and moves nothing.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"}],"tierProbed":"advanced","evidenceType":"product"}]}},"g6-bridging-w4-core":{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-core","number":7,"week":4,"track":"bridging","type":"core","title":"Misinformation Risks and Verification","summary":"Students learn why fluent AI text is not the same as true text, then name the exact claim at risk in a curated output and pair it with a concrete check against a trusted source.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["GE"],"objectives":["Explain how an AI output could spread misinformation.","Propose at least one concrete verification strategy and explain why it helps."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Misinformation Risks and Verification","body":["An AI can write an answer that sounds completely sure and is completely wrong. It is built to sound right, not to be right. Today you learn to spot the exact risk in an output and pair it with a check that makes it trustworthy before you ever share it."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can explain a specific way an AI output could be wrong and spread misinformation.","I can propose a concrete verification step and say why it makes the claim more trustworthy.","I can move from 'it might be wrong' to naming the exact risk and the exact check."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"misinformation","definition":"False information that spreads, whether or not anyone meant to deceive."},{"term":"claim","definition":"A specific statement that could be true or false, like a number or a date."},{"term":"verify","definition":"To confirm a claim against a trusted, independent source."},{"term":"trusted source","definition":"A source kept accurate by people who check facts, like an official website."},{"term":"layered check","definition":"Verifying with more than one independent source for extra reliability."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Why fluent is not the same as true","body":["An AI language system predicts the next likely word, over and over, to build fluent text. It is optimised to sound plausible, not to check facts, so it can state a wrong number as confidently as a right one.","That is what makes AI output a [[misinformation]] risk. The output gives no signal for which parts are reliable, so a made-up statistic can look exactly like a real one and spread when people repeat it."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Name the risk, then check it","body":["Good evaluation is specific. Instead of 'this might be wrong', say exactly what is at risk: the number, the name, the date, or the source.","Then pair that risk with a concrete check against a [[trusted source]] that is independent of the AI. If you can, layer two sources - one source can be wrong too."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will study three curated AI outputs. For each, name the exact risk and propose a concrete check. Your teacher will verify one live against a trusted source."],"items":["Read each output and underline the exact claim you doubt.","Name the risk type: number, date, name, or source.","Choose a trusted source that could settle it.","Write the check and why it helps.","Watch one live verification and record the verdict."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which output was the hardest to spot a risk in, and why?","Why is verifying before sharing part of using AI responsibly?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the strongest risk-and-verify pair you made today.","Note one habit you will use before sharing any AI answer."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Misinformation Risks and Verification","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-core-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"For each curated output, underline the doubtful claim and name the risk type and why it is risky.","stem":"The risky claim is ___\nThe risk type is ___\nIt is risky because ___","lookFor":"Names the exact claim at risk and a risk type - number, date, name or source - with a reason. Model response: the height figure for the landmark could be invented, because the AI predicts plausible numbers and may not have the real one. Reward a named risky element, not a general 'it might be wrong'.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-core-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"For one output, write a concrete verification strategy naming a trusted source and explaining why it reduces the risk.","stem":"I would check it by ___\nusing the trusted source ___\nThis helps because ___","lookFor":"The check must target the specific claim and name a trusted source. Model response: check the height on the landmark's official website or a reputable encyclopaedia, because those are maintained by people who verify facts. Look for the independence and reliability reasoning - an official source is independent of the AI and is kept accurate, so agreement makes the claim far more trustworthy.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-core-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Record the risk and matching check for each output you evaluated.","stem":"Columns: Output | Risk (specific claim) | Verification step | Trusted source | Why it helps","lookFor":"Mastery: each row pairs the exact claim at risk with a concrete check against a named trusted source and a clear reason it reduces the risk. Partial: a risk is identified but stays general, or the source is vague and the reason thin. Not yet: no workable check, or a check that would not address the risk.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-core-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Name one misinformation risk in an AI output and propose one concrete verification strategy, with a reason it helps.","lookFor":"Explains a plausible misinformation risk and proposes a workable verification strategy with a reason. Feedback move: push 'it could be wrong' toward a specific risk plus a specific check; extend confident students to layered verification.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-core-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Design a layered verification strategy using two independent trusted sources for one claim, and explain why two sources are more reliable than one.","lookFor":"Stretch target from the pack: designs a layered check using two independent trusted sources and explains why two beats one - a single source can also be wrong or out of date, and two genuinely independent sources rarely share the same error.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]}]}},"g6-bridging-w4-lab":{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-lab","number":8,"week":4,"track":"bridging","type":"lab","title":"Verify and De-risk Workshop (Lab)","summary":"Groups apply a fixed four-step verification routine to a batch of suspect outputs, record an evidence-backed verdict for each, then compare single-source and two-source checking to judge which is more reliable.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["GE"],"objectives":["Apply a verification routine to a set of suspect outputs and record the verdict for each.","Compare two verification strategies and judge which is more reliable."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Verify and De-risk Workshop (Lab)","body":["One suspect output is easy to check. Ten is where you need a system. Today you run a verification routine on a whole batch, reach an evidence-backed verdict on each, and then judge which checking strategy you would actually trust."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can run a verification routine on several suspect outputs and record a clear verdict for each.","I can compare two verification strategies and judge which is more reliable, with a reason.","I can back my verdicts with evidence from a trusted source."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"routine","definition":"A fixed set of steps applied the same way to every output."},{"term":"verdict","definition":"Your conclusion on a claim: verified, false, or unclear."},{"term":"evidence","definition":"The trusted-source information that backs your verdict."},{"term":"independent sources","definition":"Sources that do not copy each other, so they are unlikely to share the same error."},{"term":"reliable","definition":"A method you can trust to give the right result repeatedly."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"A routine keeps you fair","body":["When you check many outputs, it is tempting to judge each one by how confident it sounds. A verification [[routine]] stops that: the same fixed steps apply to every output, so a slick output gets no easy pass.","Our routine has four steps - isolate the claim, pick an independent trusted source, compare, and record a verdict. The [[verdict]] can be verified, false, or unclear, and 'unclear' is an honest answer when the evidence is not there."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Not all checks are equally reliable","body":["A single-source check is quick, but if that one source is wrong or out of date, your verdict is wrong too. A two-source cross-check is slower but far harder to fool, because two [[independent sources]] rarely share the same mistake.","Judging which strategy is more [[reliable]] means weighing speed against confidence and asking a sharp question: could these sources fail together? The more independent they are, the more you can trust their agreement."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Work in groups with roles: claim-isolator, source-checker, verdict-recorder, reporter. Run the routine on the whole batch, then compare two strategies on a tricky output."],"items":["Copy the four routine steps onto your sheet.","For each output, isolate the claim and pick a trusted source.","Compare and record a verdict with its evidence.","Choose one tricky output and check it single-source, then two-source.","Judge which strategy is more reliable and why.","For one false output, write the harm and the check that de-risks it."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which verdict was hardest to reach, and what made it hard?","When is the slower two-source check worth the extra time?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["State which verification strategy you would use and the trade-off you accept.","Note one responsible-use habit this workshop reinforced."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Verify and De-risk Workshop (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-lab-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Apply the four-step routine to each suspect output. Record a verdict and the trusted-source evidence for each.","stem":"The claim is ___\nMy verdict is ___\nEvidence: ___","lookFor":"Every verdict must cite the trusted source, not a feeling. Model: 'Verdict: false. The output said the tower was 900 metres; the official source lists a different height, so the claim does not match a trusted source.' Accept 'unclear' as a valid, honest verdict when no source on the reference sheet covers the claim.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-lab-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Verify one tricky output single-source, then two-source. Judge which is more reliable and explain using source independence.","stem":"Single-source result: ___\nTwo-source result: ___\nThe more reliable strategy is ___ because ___","lookFor":"Model: single-source was faster but the one source was out of date; the two-source cross-check caught the error, so it is more reliable. Look for reasoning about source independence and the speed-versus-confidence trade-off, not just which was quicker. Agreement raises confidence but is not proof if the sources could fail together.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-lab-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Log every suspect output, your verdict, the evidence, and the strategy used.","stem":"Columns: Output | Claim checked | Verdict | Evidence (trusted source) | Strategy used","lookFor":"Mastery: a verdict for each output, each backed by cited trusted-source evidence and a named strategy. Partial: verdicts for most outputs but some lack cited evidence. Not yet: verdicts are guesses with no trusted-source evidence.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-lab-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Give a verdict on one output with its evidence, and judge which verification strategy is more reliable and why.","lookFor":"Applies the routine to reach an evidence-based verdict and judges which verification strategy is more reliable with a reason. Reward evidence-backed verdicts; push students who prefer speed to weigh reliability and source independence.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]},{"id":"g6-bridging-w4-lab-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Design a three-layer verification for a high-stakes claim about a UAE record, and explain when the extra effort is worth it and when it is not.","lookFor":"Stretch target from the pack: designs a three-layer verification for a high-stakes claim and justifies when the extra effort is worth it and when it is not, reasoning about how independent the three sources really are.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}]}]}},"g6-bridging-w5-core":{"id":"g6-bridging-w5-core","number":9,"week":5,"track":"bridging","type":"core","title":"Trustworthy AI Helper - Design & Test the Data (Part 1 Core)","summary":"Students design a small AI helper from three parts - a task, a structured prompt and data - then run a clean-versus-noisy data test and explain how data quality changed the output.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Design a small prompt-driven AI helper: state the task, the structured prompt, and the data it will use.","Test how the quality of the data changes the helper's output, and explain the effect."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Over four lessons (two weeks, Core and Lab) you will build a **Trustworthy AI Helper** and prove it can be trusted.","Today you design it and test how the data quality changes its output."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can design a helper with a clear task, a structured prompt, and a data set.","I can test how data quality changes the output and explain why."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Helper","definition":"A small AI solution that completes a task using a prompt and data."},{"term":"Structured prompt","definition":"A precise instruction: goal, details, and the form of the answer."},{"term":"Data quality","definition":"How accurate, balanced, and complete the data is."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"A helper has three parts","body":["A [[helper]] is a small solution: a task, a structured prompt, and data.","Its output comes from the data and the prompt, so if the data is poor, the output is poor - even with a good prompt."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Test the data quality","body":["Feed your helper clean data, then noisy data, and compare the outputs.","This shows how much the [[data quality]] matters. Poorer data usually gives a poorer, less fair output."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Design your helper, then test clean vs noisy data."],"items":["Write the task.","Write a structured prompt.","Prepare the data.","Run on clean data; note the output.","Run on noisy data; note the output.","Explain the difference."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Why is a good prompt not enough on its own?","How did the noisy data change your output?","What will you build in the Lab?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["My helper's task is ...","Data quality mattered because ...","In the Lab I will build ..."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 5 - Core - Worksheet | Trustworthy AI Helper - Design & Test the Data (Part 1 Core)","instructions":"Name: __________________________________  Class: ________________  Date: ________________","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w5-core-q1","type":"shortText","stem":"Task: ...\nStructured prompt (goal | details | form): ...\nData: ...","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"All three parts present and clear: a single clear task (e.g. 'recommend one library book'), a structured prompt that names the goal, the details and the form of the answer (e.g. 'Using the class survey, suggest one book most pupils would enjoy, in one sentence with a reason'), and named data (e.g. the balanced reading survey). Accept any clear task + structured prompt + named data. Mastery = clear task, structured prompt and named data; partial = two of three, or one unclear.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"prompt":"Write the task, a structured prompt, and the data."},{"id":"g6-bridging-w5-core-q2","type":"shortText","stem":"Clean-data output: ...\nNoisy-data output: ...\nThe difference is because ...","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"A genuine clean-vs-noisy comparison with a stated data-quality-to-output effect, e.g. 'on clean data it suggested a popular adventure book; on noisy data (a duplicated entry) it over-suggested one title - poorer data gave a less fair output'. Look for the causal link back to the data, not just two different outputs. Watch for the misconception that a good prompt fixes bad data.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"prompt":"Record the output on clean data and on noisy data, and explain the difference."},{"id":"g6-bridging-w5-core-q3","type":"shortText","stem":"Columns: Helper part | My design | Data-quality finding","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"A completed log covering each helper part (task, structured prompt, data) with a matching data-quality finding. This is the evidence for the formative check: designs a helper with a clear task, structured prompt and data, and explains how data quality changed the output. Record CU and SD as mastery/partial/not-yet.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"prompt":"Record your design and your data-quality test."},{"id":"g6-bridging-w5-core-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"One sentence saying the output is produced from the data and the prompt, so poor data gives a poor output even when the prompt is good - the helper does not 'know' the answer. Reject answers implying a strong prompt can fix bad data.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"prompt":"Write one sentence: why does your helper need good data as well as a good prompt?"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w5-core-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"A reasoned threshold (a proportion or a number of bad entries) tied to the helper's own data and task, with reasoning about when the pattern the helper relies on would be swamped or skewed - e.g. one duplicated entry in a small survey already tips the recommendation. Reasoning matters more than the number.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"prompt":"Predict how much noise your data can take before the output becomes untrustworthy, and explain your reasoning."}]}},"g6-bridging-w5-lab":{"id":"g6-bridging-w5-lab","number":10,"week":5,"track":"bridging","type":"lab","title":"Trustworthy AI Helper - Build & Refine the Prompt (Part 1 Lab)","summary":"Students turn their Week 5 Core design into a working prototype and improve it by changing one part of the prompt at a time, logging each version, change and effect.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Build a working prototype of the helper using the structured prompt and improved data.","Refine the prompt across versions to raise output quality, logging each change and its effect."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Now build it! Today you turn your design into a working helper and make it better by refining the prompt, one change at a time."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can build a working prototype of my helper.","I can refine the prompt across versions and explain what improved."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Prototype","definition":"A first working version you can test and improve."},{"term":"Iterate","definition":"To improve something step by step, testing each change."},{"term":"Version","definition":"One attempt at your prompt that you can compare with the next."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Build, run, refine","body":["Run your prompt on the improved data and read the output.","Judge it against your task. Then change one part of the prompt and run it again.","Changing one thing at a time tells you what actually helped."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Build your prototype and refine the prompt across versions."],"items":["Run prompt v1 on your data.","Judge it against your task.","Change one part; run again.","Log the change and its effect.","Repeat until it meets the task.","Record your best version."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which change improved your output the most?","Why refine one change at a time?","Is your best prompt precise or just long?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["My best prompt version is ...","The change that helped most was ... because ...","Next I will check ..."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 5 - Lab - Worksheet | Trustworthy AI Helper - Build & Refine the Prompt (Part 1 Lab)","instructions":"Name: __________________________________  Class: ________________  Date: ________________","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w5-lab-q1","type":"shortText","stem":"v1: ... | effect: ...\nv2: ... | change: ... | effect: ...\nBest version: ...","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Distinct versions where exactly ONE part of the prompt changes between them, each with its effect, ending in a named best version. Model: v1 'suggest a book' gave a vague list; v2 added 'one book, with a reason, using the survey' and the output became specific and justified. Watch for pairs changing several things at once, or assuming a longer prompt is a better prompt.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"prompt":"Log each prompt version, the change you made, and its effect."},{"id":"g6-bridging-w5-lab-q2","type":"shortText","stem":"Columns: Version | Change made | Effect on the output","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"A complete log from v1 to the best version with a reasoned justification for the key change. This is the formative evidence for SD: iterates the prompt to a measurably better output and explains the causal reason for the improvement. For trial-and-error, prompt 'name the change and its effect'. Record SD as mastery/partial/not-yet.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"prompt":"Record each version, the change, and the effect on the output."},{"id":"g6-bridging-w5-lab-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Names a specific version, identifies a single change, and gives a causal reason for why that change improved the output (usually added detail, a named data source, or a required form). Reward a one-change improvement with a causal reason; 'it just got better' is partial.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"prompt":"Name your best prompt version and the one change that improved it most, with a reason."},{"id":"g6-bridging-w5-lab-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Recognises that detail added for accuracy can make the prompt longer and harder to follow, while a short clear prompt may leave the output vague - and names the balance they struck. Precise beats long; look for that judgement being made explicitly.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"prompt":"Refine your prompt for accuracy AND clarity at once. Explain the trade-off you had to balance."}]}},"g6-bridging-w6-core":{"id":"g6-bridging-w6-core","number":11,"week":6,"track":"bridging","type":"core","title":"Trustworthy AI Helper - Trace the Data & Plan Checks (Part 2 Core)","summary":"Students trace how the data driving their helper shapes its outcome, then plan a trusted-source verification and a risk check before trusting it.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Explain how the data used influences the helper's outcome, and predict a data change's effect.","Plan how you will verify the output and check for basic risks before trusting it."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Your helper works - now make sure it can be trusted. Today you trace how the data shapes its outcome and plan how you will verify it and check its risks."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can explain how the data shapes my helper's outcome and predict a change.","I can plan a verification and a risk check for my helper."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Trace","definition":"To follow how the data leads to the outcome."},{"term":"Verify","definition":"To check a claim against a trusted, independent source."},{"term":"Risk","definition":"A way the output could be wrong, unsafe, biased, or a privacy problem."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"The data drives the outcome","body":["Change the data and the outcome changes. So you can [[trace]] which data drives which part of the output.","Knowing this helps you trust - or fix - your helper."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Plan your checks","body":["Verification checks a claim against a trusted source - not by asking the AI again.","A [[risk]] check asks: could this be wrong, unsafe, biased, or a privacy problem, and how would I reduce it?"]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Trace the data's influence, then plan your verification and risk check."],"items":["Name the data that drives one part of your output.","Predict how a data change would alter the outcome.","Choose a claim to verify against a trusted source.","Name one risk and a way to reduce it.","Write your check plan."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["How does your data shape your outcome?","What is the most trustworthy way to verify a claim?","What is your helper's biggest risk?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["The data that most shapes my outcome is ...","I will verify ... against ...","My top risk is ... and I will reduce it by ..."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 6 - Core - Worksheet | Trustworthy AI Helper - Trace the Data & Plan Checks (Part 2 Core)","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w6-core-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Trace the data: explain how the data influences your outcome and predict a change.","stem":"The data ... drives the outcome because ...\nIf the data changed to ..., the outcome would ...","lookFor":"A specific data-to-outcome link plus a prediction - names which data drives which part of the output and says how the outcome would change if that data changed (model: the survey's most-picked genre drove the book suggestion; swapping in a class that prefers science would change the suggestion to a science book).","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"advanced"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w6-core-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Plan your checks: plan a verification and a risk check.","stem":"Claim to verify: ... | trusted source: ...\nRisk: ... | how I will reduce it: ...","lookFor":"The verification must use a trusted, independent source rather than the same tool, and the named risk must come with a mitigation (model: verify 'most pupils prefer adventure' against the survey totals; risk of a duplicate biasing the suggestion, reduced by de-duplicating the data).","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"advanced"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w6-core-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Trustworthy AI Helper Log - Part 2 (trace + check plan): record your data trace and your verification and risk plan.","stem":"Data -> outcome link | Verification plan | Risk + mitigation","lookFor":"The Core section of the Log is the assessment evidence. Mastery: explains the data's influence on the outcome, predicts a data change, and plans a trusted-source verification and a risk check with a mitigation. Partial: explanation or only one check planned.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"advanced"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w6-core-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: explain how your data shapes your outcome, and name one claim you will verify and one risk you will reduce.","lookFor":"Links the data to the outcome, names one claim to be verified against a trusted source, and names one risk together with how it will be reduced.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"advanced"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w6-core-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): find the single data change that would most alter your helper's outcome, and explain why it matters most.","lookFor":"Identifies the single data change with the largest effect and justifies it.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"advanced"}]}},"g6-bridging-w6-lab":{"id":"g6-bridging-w6-lab","number":12,"week":6,"track":"bridging","type":"lab","title":"Trustworthy AI Helper - Verify, De-risk & Showcase (Part 2 Lab)","summary":"Students carry out their verification and risk check, refine and re-verify if a check fails, then argue with evidence that their helper can be trusted and reflect across the four skills.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Carry out the verification and risk check, refine the helper if a check fails, and confirm it can be trusted.","Present the Trustworthy AI Helper and reflect on the thinking used across all four skills."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Time to prove it. Today you verify your helper, reduce a risk, and show that it can be trusted then present it and reflect on everything you have learned."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can verify my helper's output and reduce a risk, refining if needed.","I can present my helper and reflect on my thinking."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Mitigation","definition":"A change that reduces a risk."},{"term":"Trustworthy","definition":"Something you can rely on because you have checked it."},{"term":"Evidence","definition":"The proof you give for a claim or a judgement."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Verify, then de-risk","body":["Verify a claim in your output against a trusted source. If it fails, refine and re-verify.","Run your risk check and apply a [[mitigation]] - then re-check that it worked."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Verify, de-risk, then showcase and reflect."],"items":["Verify your claim against a trusted source.","If it fails, refine and re-verify.","Run your risk check; apply one mitigation.","Confirm your helper can be trusted, with evidence.","Prepare a one-minute showcase.","Present and reflect."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["What did your verification find?","What risk did you reduce, and how?","Which of the four skills was hardest?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["My helper is trustworthy because ...","The check that mattered most was ...","The skill I found hardest was ... and I worked through it by ..."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 6 - Lab - Worksheet | Trustworthy AI Helper - Verify, De-risk & Showcase (Part 2 Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-bridging-w6-lab-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Verify & de-risk: record your verification and your risk mitigation.","stem":"Claim verified: ... | source: ... | result: ...\nRisk reduced: ... | how: ... | re-check: ...","lookFor":"A trusted source, not a re-ask of the tool, and a mitigation that was actually applied and then re-checked (model: 'adventure is the most-picked genre' verified against the survey totals and it held; a duplicate entry was removed and the helper re-run, making the suggestion fairer). If a check failed, look for a refinement and a re-verify.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"advanced"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w6-lab-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Trust argument: argue, with evidence, why your helper can be trusted.","stem":"My helper can be trusted because ...","lookFor":"Argues trust from the checks actually carried out and cites that evidence, rather than asserting trust on its own.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"advanced"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w6-lab-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Trustworthy AI Helper Log - Part 2 Lab (verify, de-risk, reflect): record your checks and reflect across the four skills.","stem":"Skill used | What I did / found | How trustworthy? (evidence)","lookFor":"Completed verification and risk mitigation on the Log, with evidence, plus reflection across the four skills - data quality, prompt refinement, data influence, and verification/risk. Mastery reflection also names a next step; partial reflects on some skills only.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"advanced"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w6-lab-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: state whether your helper can be trusted and give two reasons (one verification, one risk mitigation).","lookFor":"States a judgement on whether the helper can be trusted and gives two reasons, one verification and one risk mitigation.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"advanced"},{"id":"g6-bridging-w6-lab-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): your helper is safe for one use but risky for another. Explain the difference and propose a rule for when it should and should not be used.","lookFor":"Weighs a fairness/safety trade-off: distinguishes the safe use from the risky one and proposes a workable rule for when the helper should and should not be used.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CU·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·SD·G5·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":4,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·CE·G4·A"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":5,"tier":"A","code":"AIF·GE·G5·A"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"advanced"}]}},"g6-main-w1-core":{"id":"g6-main-w1-core","number":1,"week":1,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Unit Launch - Rule-Based vs Learning Systems","summary":"Students meet the term's big question by sorting three real systems into 'follows fixed rules' or 'learns from data', posting their own questions and helping agree the class responsible-use rules.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Generate at least two questions about how rule-based and learning systems differ.","Classify three systems as 'follows fixed rules' or 'learns from data', justifying each.","Co-construct the unit success criteria and the responsible-use norms for AI tools."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Some machines are told every single step. Others are shown lots of examples and work out the pattern themselves. This term you become a system detective - can you tell which kind you are looking at?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can ask at least two genuine questions about rule-based and learning systems.","I can sort three systems into 'follows fixed rules' or 'learns from data' and give a reason for each.","I can help write our class responsible-use rules for AI tools."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"rule-based system","definition":"A system that follows fixed steps a person wrote for it."},{"term":"learning system","definition":"A system that finds patterns from many examples instead of fixed rules."},{"term":"data","definition":"The examples or information a learning system is shown."},{"term":"model","definition":"The pattern a learning system builds from its data."},{"term":"example","definition":"One piece of data, like one photo or one email."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Two kinds of machine","body":["A [[rule-based system]] is like a recipe. A person writes every step, and the machine follows them exactly. A Salik toll gate charges you when your car passes - a person set that rule.","A [[learning system]] is different. Nobody writes a rule for every case. Instead it is shown many examples - this is [[data]] - and it finds the pattern by itself. The pattern it builds is called a [[model]]."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Why it matters","body":["If you know which kind a system is, you can guess when it might fail. A rule-based system fails when something happens that has no rule. A learning system can fail if it was shown poor or unfair examples.","Neither kind is magic, and neither understands like a person. That is why we always check what a machine tells us."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Time to be a detective. Sort three systems, then post the questions you most want answered this term."],"items":["Watch the two clips your teacher shows and spot which machine was told every step.","Sort the three cards into 'fixed rules' or 'learns from data'.","Write a reason under each card.","Post two questions on the class question wall.","Help agree one class rule for safe AI use."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which system was hardest to sort, and why?","What is one clue that a system learned from examples?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write one thing you now understand about how machines make decisions.","Write one question you still have."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 1 - Core - Worksheet | Unit Launch - Rule-Based vs Learning Systems","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w1-core-m-w1-core-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"title":"Sort three systems","prompt":"Write each system in the correct column and give one reason.","stem":"This follows fixed rules because ___\nThis learns from data because ___","lookFor":"Sorts at least 2 of the 3 systems correctly with a sensible reason. The traffic-light timer is fixed rules - it changes lights on a set timer that a person programmed; accept 'learns' only if the student describes a smart signal that adapts to traffic data. The spam-email filter learns from data - it was trained on many example emails marked spam or not spam, and no person wrote a rule for every email.","evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w1-core-m-w1-core-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"title":"My two questions","prompt":"Write two genuine questions about rule-based and learning systems for the question wall.","lookFor":"Poses at least two genuine, on-topic questions, for example 'How many examples does a system need before it learns well?'. Any genuine, on-topic question counts - reward curiosity over neatness.","evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w1-core-m-w1-core-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"title":"System sort record","prompt":"Complete the table for the three systems you sorted. For each row record: System | Fixed rules or learns from data | My reason.","lookFor":"Each row carries a reason, not a guess - press for where the logic comes from. Model rows: traffic-light timer, fixed rules, 'it changes lights on a set timer that a person programmed'; spam-email filter, learns from data, 'it was trained on many example emails marked spam or not spam'. Accept 'learns' for the traffic-light timer only if the student describes a smart signal that adapts to traffic data.","evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w1-core-m-w1-core-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"title":"Exit ticket","prompt":"In one sentence, how is a learning system different from a rule-based system?","lookFor":"Names where the logic comes from: a rule-based system does exactly what a person has written into it step by step, while a learning system is shown many examples and finds the pattern in that data. Watch for the misconception that a learning system is just a big set of fixed rules a programmer typed in.","evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w1-core-m-w1-core-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"title":"Challenge (stretch)","prompt":"Invent your own system card and swap with a partner - can they classify and justify it?","lookFor":"Invents a fourth system card that can genuinely be classified, and challenges another pair to classify and justify it. Strong responses can explain which clue in their own card settles the classification.","evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]}]}},"g6-main-w1-lab":{"id":"g6-main-w1-lab","number":2,"week":1,"track":"main","type":"lab","title":"Rule vs Learning - Hands-On Exploration (Lab)","summary":"Working unplugged at two stations, students run a fixed rule-card machine and a learn-from-examples card game, log the differences and design a test that would reveal which kind an unknown system is.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Explore a rule-based demo and a simple learning demo and log the differences you observe.","Devise a test that could tell whether a system follows rules or learns.","Predict what would break each system and check your prediction."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Today you get your hands on two machines - one that never changes and one that learns from examples. Watch closely: their differences will jump out."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can run the rule-card machine and the learn-from-examples game and record what each does.","I can log at least three differences I observed between them.","I can design a fair test that would reveal whether a system follows rules or learns."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"rule-card machine","definition":"A fixed stack of if-then cards that always behaves the same way."},{"term":"example card","definition":"A labelled card the group learns a pattern from."},{"term":"pattern","definition":"The rule-of-thumb your group builds from the example cards."},{"term":"test input","definition":"A new input you give a system to see how it behaves."},{"term":"predictable","definition":"Behaves the same way every time for the same input."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Two stations, two behaviours","body":["At Station A the [[rule-card machine]] never changes. Feed the same input twice and you get the same output twice. That is a rule-based system.","At Station B nobody gives you rules. You study [[example card]]s, build a [[pattern]], then classify new cards. That is a learning system in miniature."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Design a fair test","body":["To tell the two apart, give each a brand-new, unusual input. A rule-based machine reacts the same as always or breaks. A learning system tries to match the pattern it built.","The best detectives write down exactly what they will try and what each result would mean."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Run both stations, log the differences, then design a test that would reveal which kind an unknown machine is."],"items":["Take a role: feeder, recorder or tester.","Run Station A with five inputs, including one strange one.","Run Station B: study examples, then sort five new cards.","Log at least three differences.","Write one test that would separate rules from learning."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which station was more predictable, and how did you know?","What made the learning game guess wrongly?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the clearest difference you saw today.","Write what you would test first on a mystery machine."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 1 - Lab - Worksheet | Rule vs Learning - Hands-On Exploration (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w1-lab-m-w1-lab-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"title":"Station comparison","prompt":"Log what each station did for the same kind of input.","stem":"Station A always ___\nStation B changed when ___","lookFor":"Records behaviour actually observed, e.g. 'Station A always gave the same output; Station B's guesses changed as we saw more example cards.' Any accurate observed difference is valid.","evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w1-lab-m-w1-lab-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"title":"Design a test","prompt":"Write a test that would tell whether a system follows rules or learns, and what each result would mean.","lookFor":"Designs a test that would truly separate rules from learning: give the system a brand-new, unusual input; if it always reacts the same way it is rule-based, if it adapts using patterns it likely learned. Accept any test using a novel input to reveal adaptation; if the test does not separate the two, model one strong test and have the group revise.","evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w1-lab-m-w1-lab-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"title":"Difference log","prompt":"Record the differences you observed between the two stations. For each row record: What we tried | Station A did | Station B did.","lookFor":"Logs at least three accurate differences with detail rather than one repeated observation. Look for the unusual input at Station A being noted: it had no rule for it, so it stopped or gave a wrong answer.","evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w1-lab-m-w1-lab-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"title":"Exit ticket","prompt":"What one input would you give a mystery machine to find out if it learns? Why?","lookFor":"Chooses a brand-new or unusual input and says what each outcome would mean - the same reaction every time points to fixed rules, while an attempt to match a pattern points to learning. A named input with a reason beats a general statement about testing.","evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w1-lab-m-w1-lab-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"title":"Challenge (stretch)","prompt":"Add an unfair set of example cards to Station B and explain how it changed the pattern the group learned.","lookFor":"Adds a deliberately unfair set of example cards and explains how it changed the learned pattern. Strong responses reach the idea that quality and fairness of examples matter, not just how many there are - a few odd or wrong cards can make the guesses worse.","evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]}]}},"g6-main-w10-core":{"id":"g6-main-w10-core","number":19,"week":10,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Showcase, Assessment & Reflection","summary":"Learners present their finished solution with two justified choices, complete a short Term-1 assessment sampling all four strands, and set a goal for next term.","duration":"1 x 45-minute period","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Present the finished solution and explain at least two design/evaluation choices.","Demonstrate the term's key outcomes in a short assessment task.","State one thing learned and set one goal for next term."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["This is your moment to show what you built and what you learned. You will present your finished solution, explain the choices behind it, complete a short assessment, and set a goal to grow even more next term."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can present my finished solution and explain at least two design or evaluation choices.","I can show the term's key outcomes in a short assessment task.","I can state one thing I learned and set one goal for next term."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Pitch","definition":"A short, clear explanation of your solution and its value."},{"term":"Justify","definition":"To give the reason and evidence behind a choice."},{"term":"Assessment","definition":"A task that shows what you have learned across the strands."},{"term":"Reflection","definition":"Thinking about what worked and what you would do next."},{"term":"Goal","definition":"A clear target for your next step of learning."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"A pitch that tells the truth","body":["A strong [[pitch]] names your user, the problem, how your solution works, and the evidence that it is accurate, useful and safe.","Explain two choices by giving reasons, not just descriptions. Be honest about one limit. Honesty makes your work more trustworthy, not weaker."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Show the whole term, then look ahead","body":["The short [[assessment]] lets you show all four strands: understanding, building, evaluating and using responsibly, and wider effects.","Finish by naming one thing you learned and setting a [[goal]] for next term, aiming toward the Proficient tier."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Complete the short assessment, then present your solution and justify two choices before setting your next-term goal."],"items":["Set out your evidence from the term.","Complete the short assessment task on your own.","Prepare a 60-second pitch with two justified choices.","Present to your group and answer one 'why' question.","Write one thing learned and one Term-2 goal."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which of your two choices are you proudest of, and why?","What limit of your solution is important to be honest about?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write one thing you learned this term.","Set one goal for Term 2 that moves you toward Proficient."]}],"worksheet":{"questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w10-core-q1","type":"shortText","prompt":"Write two design or evaluation choices and give a reason and evidence for each.","title":"My two justified choices","stem":"Choice 1: ... because ... evidence ...\nChoice 2: ... because ... evidence ...","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Explains two design/evaluation choices with reasons and evidence. For a design choice, look for a reason, not just a description (model: 'I used a rule-based check because recycling categories have fixed correct answers'). For an evaluation choice, it should link to accuracy or fairness (model: 'I tested with six known-answer cases because one lucky example is not enough evidence').","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w10-core-q2","type":"shortText","prompt":"Answer the four short prompts, one for each strand, at Emerging level.","title":"Term-1 assessment task","stem":"Concepts: ...\nDesign & build: ...\nEvaluate & use: ...\nWider effects: ...","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Shows each strand at Emerging in the task. Approaching shows most strands at Emerging; not yet means several strands are not yet shown.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w10-core-q3","type":"shortText","prompt":"For each strand, note your evidence and your confidence.","title":"Strand evidence tracker","stem":"Strand | My evidence | Confidence | Next step","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Evidence is the assessed task plus the reflection, with results reported per strand. Feedback flags each student's next tier target (to Proficient) for Term 2.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w10-core-q4","type":"shortText","prompt":"One thing I learned this term is ... and my goal for next term is ...","title":"Exit ticket","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"States one learning and one clear next-term goal. Goal should aim toward Proficient (model: 'I learned to re-test after changes; my goal is to build a solution with balanced data next term').","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w10-core-q5","type":"shortText","prompt":"Explain a trade-off you made between two choices and why it was worth it.","title":"Challenge (stretch)","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Stretch / next tier: learners explain a trade-off between two choices, reaching Extended abstract thinking.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]}]}},"g6-main-w10-lab":{"id":"g6-main-w10-lab","number":20,"week":10,"track":"main","type":"lab","title":"Showcase Carousel and Reflection (Lab)","summary":"Learners run a showcase carousel, give and collect strand-tagged peer feedback, and consolidate the term into a four-strand Emerging summary with one Term-2 goal.","duration":"1 x 45-minute period","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Present the solution in a showcase carousel and give structured peer feedback.","Consolidate reflections into a Term-2 goal across the four strands.","Confirm the responsible-use message for every solution on display."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Welcome to the showcase. Today your class becomes a gallery of AI-supported solutions. You will present, learn from each other with honest feedback, and pull your whole term together into one clear goal for next time."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can present my solution in a showcase carousel and give structured peer feedback.","I can consolidate my reflections into a Term-2 goal across the four strands.","I can confirm my solution is responsible to use and honest about its limits."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Carousel","definition":"A rotation where you present at your station and visit others."},{"term":"Two stars and a wish","definition":"Two things done well and one thing to improve."},{"term":"Consolidate","definition":"To gather many notes into one clear summary."},{"term":"Responsible use","definition":"No personal data, honest about limits, fair to users."},{"term":"Proficient","definition":"The next tier of skill you aim for in Term 2."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Feedback that helps","body":["In the [[carousel]] you present in 60 seconds and then visit other stations. Leave '[[two stars and a wish]]', and tag each comment to a strand.","Good feedback is specific, kind and tied to evidence. A helpful wish is a gift, not a criticism."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Your term in one picture","body":["Gather your feedback and notes into the four-strand summary: what you can now do in concepts, design and build, evaluate and use, and wider effects.","Then set one specific Term-2 goal toward [[Proficient]], and confirm every solution is responsible to use."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Set up your station, present in the carousel, give and collect feedback, then consolidate your term into a four-strand summary and one goal."],"items":["Set up your station with your frozen solution, evidence and responsible-use message.","Present in 60 seconds with two justified choices.","Rotate to at least two stations and leave strand-tagged feedback.","Collect the feedback left at your station.","Complete the four-strand Emerging summary.","Set one Term-2 goal and confirm responsible use."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["What is the most useful wish someone gave you, and why?","How will you keep your future solutions responsible and fair?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write your four-strand summary, one line per strand.","Set one specific Term-2 goal that moves you toward Proficient."]}],"worksheet":{"questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w10-lab-q1","type":"shortText","prompt":"For two peers, write two stars and a wish, each tagged to a strand.","title":"Carousel feedback slips","stem":"Star (strand): ...\nStar (strand): ...\nWish (strand): ...","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Gives specific 'two stars and a wish' tagged to strands. A star must name a strand and evidence (model: 'Star (Evaluate & use): you tested with six known-answer cases, which is strong evidence'). A wish should be specific and kind (model: 'Wish (Wider effects): explain who might be treated unfairly and how you would prevent it').","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w10-lab-q2","type":"shortText","prompt":"Write one Emerging statement per strand, then set one Term-2 goal.","title":"Four-strand Emerging summary and goal","stem":"Concepts: I can ...\nDesign & build: I can ...\nEvaluate & use: I can ...\nWider effects: I can ...\nTerm-2 goal: ...","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Summarises all four strands at Emerging with evidence - one clause per strand at Emerging (model: 'I can explain rule-based vs learning, build a solution, evaluate it for risk, and consider wider effects'). The goal should be specific and tied to a strand toward Proficient (model: 'Next term I will use balanced data so my solution is fairer to all users').","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w10-lab-q3","type":"shortText","prompt":"For each strand, record your Emerging evidence and next-term focus.","title":"End-of-term reflection tracker","stem":"Strand | Emerging evidence | Feedback received | Term-2 focus","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Evidence is the feedback slips plus the completed four-strand Emerging summary with goal. Teacher flags each learner's next-tier target for Term 2.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w10-lab-q4","type":"shortText","prompt":"My proudest strand this term is ... and my Term-2 goal toward Proficient is ...","title":"Exit ticket","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Sets a specific goal and confirms responsible use; setting a goal or confirming responsible use but not both is only Approaching.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w10-lab-q5","type":"shortText","prompt":"Combine feedback from several peers into one improved design idea to try in Term 2, and explain your choice.","title":"Challenge (stretch)","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Stretch / next tier: learners synthesise feedback from several peers into one improved design idea for Term 2.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]}]}},"g6-main-w2-core":{"id":"g6-main-w2-core","number":3,"week":2,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Rules vs Learning - How Each System Works","summary":"Two features - where the logic comes from and how a system handles something new - let you classify any everyday system as rule-based or learning.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU"],"objectives":["Distinguish a rule-based system from a learning system using two clear features.","Give one everyday example of each and explain the difference.","Use the terms rule-based, learning, data and model accurately."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Two machines can do the same job in totally different ways. Today you learn the two features that reveal which is which - and you will never be fooled by a shiny screen again."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"rule-based system","definition":"Logic written by a person as fixed steps."},{"term":"learning system","definition":"A system that builds a model from data instead of fixed rules."},{"term":"data","definition":"The labelled examples a learning system is shown."},{"term":"model","definition":"The pattern a learning system builds from its data."},{"term":"feature","definition":"A true characteristic you use to classify a system, not a surface look."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Two features that decide it","body":["**Feature one** - where does the logic come from? In a [[rule-based system]] a person writes it. In a [[learning system]] it comes from data.","**Feature two** - what happens with something new? A rule-based system only handles cases its rules cover. A learning system can handle new cases that look like its examples."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Data is not the model","body":["Be careful with words. The [[data]] is the examples. The [[model]] is the pattern built from them. Change the data and you can change the model.","A learning system does not understand meaning - it matches patterns. Always check its answers."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Build the feature chart, then use it to classify everyday systems you meet in the UAE."],"items":["Copy the two-column feature chart.","Add an example to each row.","Classify two everyday systems.","Underline the feature that decided each answer.","Write two sentences using data and model correctly."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which feature is the strongest proof of a learning system?","Why is 'it looks modern' not a good reason?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the two features you now use to classify systems.","Write one system you are still unsure about and why."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 2 Core worksheet - Rules vs Learning - How Each System Works","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w2-core-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Feature chart. Complete the chart with a feature and an example for each system type.","stem":"Rule-based: logic comes from ___\nLearning: logic comes from ___","lookFor":"Both rows name a non-surface feature - that in a rule-based system a person writes the logic, and in a learning system the logic comes from data - and each row carries an example. Watch for surface traits such as 'it looks modern' standing in for a feature.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w2-core-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Classify and justify. Classify two systems and underline the deciding feature.","stem":"This is a ___ system because ___","lookFor":"Classifies both examples correctly with a feature reason: a metro ticket gate is rule-based because a person set fixed rules, and a voice assistant that improves with use is learning because it builds a model from data and handles new phrases. Approaching: one classified correctly, or a surface trait mixed in.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w2-core-q3","type":"fillBlank","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Classification record. Record each system, your classification, and the deciding feature.","blanks":["System","Rule-based or learning","Deciding feature"],"lookFor":"Each row pairs a classification with a deciding feature rather than a surface look. The deciding-feature column should say where the logic comes from or how the system handles a new case.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w2-core-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket. Classify two systems your teacher names and give the feature that separates them.","lookFor":"Secure: classifies both correctly and names a distinguishing feature that is not a surface trait. Sort exit slips secure / partial / not-yet; if under 70 percent distinguish correctly, re-teach with the two-column feature chart.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w2-core-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch). Find a system that is genuinely hard to classify and argue both sides using features.","lookFor":"Argues both sides using the two features - where the logic comes from, and how the system handles a new case - rather than surface traits. This is the stretch task for students who already classify securely.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"proficient","evidenceType":"product"}]}},"g6-main-w2-lab":{"id":"g6-main-w2-lab","number":4,"week":2,"track":"main","type":"lab","title":"Train a Simple Model (Lab)","summary":"Groups build a training set of safe, non-personal examples, train a classifier, test it on held-back examples, and check how their choices shaped the model.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU"],"objectives":["Train a simple classifier on non-personal examples (objects/drawings/sounds) and test it on new examples.","Explain how the training examples shaped what the model learned.","Run a basic risk and data-safety check on the training set."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Today you become a model trainer. You choose the examples, and the examples decide what your model learns - so choose wisely and keep them safe."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"training data","definition":"The examples the model learns from."},{"term":"testing data","definition":"New examples, held back, used to check the model."},{"term":"model","definition":"The pattern the system builds from the training data."},{"term":"classifier","definition":"A model that sorts examples into categories."},{"term":"data-safety rule","definition":"Use only non-personal examples - never faces, voices or names."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Train, then test","body":["First you show the model many labelled [[training data]] examples. It finds the pattern and builds a [[model]].","Then you test it on new examples it has never seen. If it still gets them right, it really learned. If not, it only memorised."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Your examples shape the model","body":["If all your apple pictures are red, the model learns 'red', not 'apple', and fails on a green one. Variety and fairness matter, not just how many.","**Golden rule:** only non-personal examples. No faces, no voices, no names ever go into a training set."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Build a training set of safe, non-personal examples, train a model, then test it on new examples and check your data is safe."],"items":["Pick two categories of non-personal items.","Collect and label 8-10 training examples each.","Hide 3 testing examples per category.","Train using training examples only.","Test the held-back examples and log the results.","Complete the data-safety and risk check."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which testing example fooled the model, and why?","How did your choice of examples change what it learned?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write how your training examples shaped the model.","Write one data-safety rule you followed today."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 2 Lab worksheet - Train a Simple Model","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w2-lab-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Build and test log. Record your categories, then predict and test each held-back example.","stem":"I predict this will be classified as ___\nThe model actually classified it as ___","lookFor":"Secure: the two categories are named, the prediction is written before the example is tested, and the tested examples are ones held back from training. Approaching: trains and tests but blurs the two sets.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w2-lab-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Data-safety and risk check. Tick the safety checks and write one risk and one improvement.","stem":"My data was safe because ___\nOne risk was ___\nOne improvement is ___","lookFor":"The safety line must confirm non-personal data - all examples were objects or drawings, no faces, voices or names. A secure improvement links quality and variety to accuracy, for example adding varied examples in different sizes, colours or angles so the model learns the real category.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w2-lab-q3","type":"fillBlank","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Training and testing record. Log each testing example, your prediction, the result, and whether it was correct.","blanks":["Testing example","Predicted","Model result","Correct?"],"lookFor":"Every logged example is one the model never saw in training, and prediction and result are recorded separately so a wrong prediction is still evidence. If a group has logged training examples here, re-model the split and have them re-run on held-back data.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w2-lab-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket. How did your training examples change what the model learned? Give one example.","lookFor":"Reward any accurate cause-and-effect from data to model, for example 'all our circles were large, so the model missed a small circle - it learned large as well as round'. Partial: an explanation with no named example.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w2-lab-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch). Design a 'trick' testing example, predict whether the model will fail, then test it and explain the result.","lookFor":"Predicts whether the model will fail and why before testing, then explains the result against that prediction - the stretch task for groups whose training and testing log is already secure.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"proficient","evidenceType":"product"}]}},"g6-main-w3-core":{"id":"g6-main-w3-core","number":5,"week":3,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Structured Prompts to Complete a Task","summary":"Students learn the Goal-Context-Format prompt structure, use it to complete a defined task on a teacher-operated tool, judge whether the task was completed, and make one adjustment to complete it more fully.","duration":"1 x 45-minute period","strands":["SD"],"objectives":["Use a structured prompt to complete a defined task with an AI tool.","Describe the input structure used and whether the task was completed.","Adjust the prompt once to complete the task more fully."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Structured Prompts to Complete a Task","body":["Ask an AI tool a vague question and you get a vague answer. Learn to structure your prompt and you can actually get a task done. Today you learn the trick: **Goal, Context, Format**."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can write a structured prompt with a clear goal, context and format.","I can say whether the task was completed and why.","I can improve my prompt once to complete the task more fully."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"prompt","definition":"The instruction you give an AI tool."},{"term":"structured prompt","definition":"A prompt with a clear goal, context and format."},{"term":"goal","definition":"What you want the tool to do."},{"term":"context","definition":"The details the tool needs to do it."},{"term":"format","definition":"How you want the answer to look."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Goal, Context, Format","body":["A vague prompt like 'tell me about the desert' leaves the tool guessing. A [[structured prompt]] tells it exactly what to do.","**Goal**: what you want. **Context**: the details it needs. **Format**: how the answer should look. Together they make the task far more likely to be completed."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Check, then improve","body":["Once you get an output, compare it to the task. Did it do everything you asked? If not, change one thing in your prompt and try again.","Two safety rules: never put personal data in a prompt, and always verify the answer - a confident output can still be wrong."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Write a structured prompt for a real task, judge whether it completed the task, and improve it once."],"items":["Underline what 'complete' means for your task.","Write your prompt with Goal, Context and Format.","Watch the teacher run it on the approved tool.","Judge whether the task was completed.","Adjust your prompt once and verify the output."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which part of the structure helped most?","How did you know the task was or was not complete?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the one adjustment that improved your result.","Write one safety rule you used today."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 3 - Core - Worksheet | Structured Prompts to Complete a Task","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w3-core-q1","type":"shortText","prompt":"Write a structured prompt","stem":"Goal: ___\nContext: ___\nFormat: ___","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"All three parts present and specific: Goal (what to do), Context (details the tool needs), Format (how the answer should look). Emerging (expected) is a prompt with a clear goal, context and format; two of the three parts is approaching; a vague or unstructured prompt is not yet. Structure beats length - a short, clear frame can be stronger than a long rambling one. No personal or identifying data in the context.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w3-core-q2","type":"shortText","prompt":"Evaluate and adjust","stem":"Task complete? ___ because ___\nMy one adjustment: ___","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Look for judgement against the task, not just 'it looks good' - for example 'Not fully - it gave four bullets but I asked for three, and one was off-topic.' The adjustment should be one clear change that improves completion, such as adding 'Give exactly three bullet points, each under 12 words.' Emerging (expected): judges completion accurately against the task and makes one adjustment that improves completion.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w3-core-q3","type":"shortText","prompt":"Task-completion log","columns":["Structured prompt","Output summary","Task complete?","One adjustment"],"autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"This is the lesson's main evidence. The log should show a structured prompt, a summary of the output, a 'task complete?' judgement made against the task, and one adjustment that improved completion. Accept a partly completed task if the judgement and adjustment are honest and specific.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w3-core-q4","type":"shortText","prompt":"Exit ticket","stem":"What are the three parts of a structured prompt, and which helped you most today?","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Goal (what to do), Context (details the tool needs), Format (how the answer should look). All three must be present, in the student's own wording and in any order or layout. The second half is a personal judgement - accept any one of the three parts as long as a reason is given.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w3-core-q5","type":"shortText","prompt":"Challenge (stretch)","stem":"Add an 'Audience' part to your prompt and describe how it changed the output.","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Stretch task: the student adds a fourth element (Audience) and shows how it improved the output. Look for a named audience in the prompt and a described change in the output that can be traced to it.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"}]}]}},"g6-main-w3-lab":{"id":"g6-main-w3-lab","number":6,"week":3,"track":"main","type":"lab","title":"Prompt-to-Task Workshop (Lab)","summary":"Students tackle a richer multi-requirement task with two different prompt structures, compare them on completion and accuracy, and turn the stronger one into a reusable template with a verification step and risk check built in.","duration":"1 x 45-minute period","strands":["SD"],"objectives":["Complete a second, richer task and compare which prompt structure worked better.","Design a reusable prompt structure for a class of tasks.","Evaluate the outputs for accuracy and run a basic risk check."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Prompt-to-Task Workshop (Lab)","body":["You can structure one prompt - now build something reusable. Today you compare prompt structures on a richer task and design a template you and your class can use again and again."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can complete a richer task with a structured prompt and compare two structures.","I can design a reusable prompt template for similar tasks.","I can check an output for accuracy and complete a risk check."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"richer task","definition":"A task with several requirements to complete."},{"term":"prompt structure","definition":"The order and parts of a prompt, such as Goal-Context-Format."},{"term":"reusable template","definition":"A prompt skeleton with slots that works for many similar tasks."},{"term":"verification","definition":"Checking an output against a reliable source."},{"term":"risk check","definition":"A quick check for personal data and possible errors."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Compare, then reuse","body":["A [[richer task]] has several requirements, so a weak prompt structure leaves parts unfinished. Run two structures on the same task and compare which completes it and which is more accurate.","Then turn the winner into a [[reusable template]] - a prompt with labelled slots you can fill in for any similar task."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Build in the checks","body":["Every good template forces two checks: verify the output against a reliable source, and run a [[risk check]] for personal data and errors.","This is how real AI solutions are designed - and it is the foundation for your signature activity in the coming weeks."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Complete a richer task with two structures, compare them, then design a reusable template with checks built in."],"items":["List the three requirements of the task.","Draft two structured prompts with different orders.","Compare which output completed the task and check accuracy.","Turn the stronger structure into a reusable template.","Add a verification step and a risk check."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which structure completed more of the task, and how do you know?","Where should the verify step sit in your template?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the biggest difference between your two structures.","Write how your template forces an accuracy check."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 3 - Lab - Worksheet | Prompt-to-Task Workshop (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w3-lab-q1","type":"shortText","prompt":"Compare two structures","stem":"Structure A met ___ of 3\nStructure B met ___ of 3\nThe stronger structure was ___ because ___","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"The comparison must be on completion or accuracy, not length - for example 'Structure A met all three requirements; Structure B missed the tone, so A was stronger.' Emerging (expected): compares two structures on completion and accuracy with evidence. Watch for the misconception that the longest answer is the best.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w3-lab-q2","type":"shortText","prompt":"Design a reusable template","stem":"Goal: ___\nContext (no personal data): ___\nFormat: ___\nVerify: ___","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Model template: 'Goal: [task]; Context: [details, no personal data]; Format: [exact format]; then Verify: check facts against a source.' The template must include a verify/risk step and use labelled slots rather than one fixed task. Emerging (expected): a clear reusable template with labelled slots.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w3-lab-q3","type":"shortText","prompt":"Structure comparison record","columns":["Structure","Requirements met","Accurate?","Stronger?"],"autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Evidence for the comparison: both structures recorded, requirements met marked per structure, an accuracy check against a reliable source, and a stated winner. A record that marks completion but never checks accuracy is only approaching - sounding right is not being right.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w3-lab-q4","type":"shortText","prompt":"Exit ticket","stem":"Write your reusable template in one line and say how it checks accuracy.","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"A one-line template with labelled slots (Goal / Context / Format) plus a named verification step - checking the output against a reliable source before use. Privacy (no personal data requested) is a bonus here; both privacy and accuracy must appear in the risk check overall.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w3-lab-q5","type":"shortText","prompt":"Challenge (stretch)","stem":"Test your template on a genuinely different task and note where it needed changing.","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Stretch task: groups test the template on a genuinely different task type and report where it needed changing. Look for the insight that a template is a strong starting point, not a guarantee - each new task still needs its output verified.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"}]}]}},"g6-main-w4-core":{"id":"g6-main-w4-core","number":7,"week":4,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Judging Outputs - Correct, Incorrect, Unclear","summary":"Students learn to give every AI output one of three verdicts - correct, incorrect or unclear - and to back each judgement with a reason they can check.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CE"],"objectives":["Identify, in a set of outputs, which are incorrect or unclear and say why.","Explain one signal that an output cannot be trusted as-is.","Recommend one action to take when an output is unclear."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Judging Outputs - Correct, Incorrect, Unclear","body":["An answer can sound perfectly sure of itself and still be wrong. Today you become the judge who decides whether an output is correct, incorrect, or simply unclear - and you learn to back up every judgement with a reason."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can sort outputs into correct, incorrect, and unclear.","I can give a reason for each judgement I make.","I can name one action to take when an output cannot be trusted."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Output","definition":"The text or answer an AI tool produces after you give it a prompt."},{"term":"Verdict","definition":"The judgement you give an output: correct, incorrect, or unclear."},{"term":"Source","definition":"A trusted place, like a textbook or official website, you check an answer against."},{"term":"Hallucination","definition":"When an AI tool states something made up as if it were a fact."},{"term":"Verify","definition":"To check whether something is true using a reliable source."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Sounds right is not the same as is right","body":["A language model writes by predicting which words usually come next. That makes its answers smooth and confident, but confidence is a writing style, not proof.","Your job is to separate 'sounds right' from 'is right' by checking the output against the task you set and a source you trust."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Three verdicts","body":["**Correct** means the output matches the task and agrees with a trusted source.","**Incorrect** means it clashes with something you can check now - a fact, a number, or your instruction.","**Unclear** means it is vague, incomplete, or you cannot [[verify]] it yet, so it needs more checking before use."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will triage three short outputs. For each, give a verdict and a six-word reason. Remember: a reason must point to a check, not a feeling."],"items":["Read the task the output was meant to answer.","Read the output carefully.","Check it against the task and any source given.","Write your verdict: correct, incorrect, or unclear.","Add a short reason and, if needed, one action."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which signal most often warned you an output was not trustworthy?","When an output is unclear, is it safer to pause or to proceed?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write one thing you will now check before trusting an AI answer.","Describe a time a confident answer turned out to be wrong."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 4 - Core - Worksheet | Judging Outputs - Correct, Incorrect, Unclear","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w4-core-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Triage three outputs. For each output, circle a verdict and write a checkable reason.","stem":"This output is ... because when I checked it against ...\nI cannot verify this because ...","lookFor":"Triages at least 2 of the 3 outputs correctly, and every verdict carries a reason tied to the task or to a source rather than to a feeling.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w4-core-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Signal and action. Name one signal that an output cannot be trusted, and the action you would take.","stem":"A warning signal is ...\nMy action would be ...","lookFor":"Any one checkable signal is acceptable - it contradicts the task, has no source, or contains a detail we cannot check - paired with a sensible action such as re-check, re-prompt, or ask a person.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w4-core-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Output-triage record. Record your verdict, reason, and action for each output.","stem":"Output | Verdict | Reason (checkable) | Action","lookFor":"Each row pairs a verdict with a reason tied to task or source, and names a sensible action for any output judged unclear or incorrect.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w4-core-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: flag these three outputs correct / incorrect / unclear and give a reason for each.","lookFor":"Correctly triages at least 2 of 3 outputs and gives a valid reason for each judgement. Push any 'it just feels wrong' towards a checkable reason.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w4-core-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): find the single detail in a 'correct-looking' output that actually makes it incorrect, and explain how you spotted it.","lookFor":"Isolates the one subtle detail that flips the output from correct to incorrect, and explains the check that exposed it rather than describing a general impression.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"}]}},"g6-main-w4-lab":{"id":"g6-main-w4-lab","number":8,"week":4,"track":"main","type":"lab","title":"Output Triage Clinic (Lab)","summary":"Groups rotate through four output stations, diagnose each as correct, incorrect or unclear, choose a verification action, and rank them all on a trust ladder they must defend.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CE"],"objectives":["Apply a triage routine to a set of curated outputs and record correct/incorrect/unclear with reasons.","Rank outputs from most to least trustworthy and defend the order.","Choose a verification action for each untrustworthy output."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Output Triage Clinic (Lab)","body":["Welcome to the triage clinic. Each output is a patient waiting for a diagnosis - correct, incorrect, or unclear - and a treatment: the action you would take to verify it. By the end you will have ranked them all on a trust ladder."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can run the triage routine across a full set of outputs.","I can rank outputs by trustworthiness and defend my order.","I can choose a verification action for outputs I cannot trust."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Triage","definition":"Quickly sorting items by how they need to be handled."},{"term":"Verification action","definition":"The concrete step you take to check whether an output is true."},{"term":"Trust ladder","definition":"An ordering of outputs from most to least trustworthy."},{"term":"Risk check","definition":"Asking what could go wrong if an output were used as-is."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Match the check to the output","body":["A fact needs a trusted source. A summary needs comparing back to the original text. A vague instruction needs a clearer prompt before you can even judge it.","Choosing the right check is what turns a guess into a verified judgement."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Rank and defend","body":["After triaging, place your outputs on the [[trust ladder]] from most to least trustworthy.","Be ready to defend your order using signals, not preferences: what evidence puts one output above another?"]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Rotate through four stations. Triage each output, choose a verification action, then rank them all on the trust ladder and prepare to defend your order."],"items":["Read the task and output at each station.","Assign a verdict and write a checkable reason.","Choose a verification action that fits the output type.","Rank all outputs on the trust ladder.","Agree your group's defence and one risk of the least trustworthy output."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which verification action was hardest to choose, and why?","Did any output move up or down the ladder after you discussed it?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write one verification action you will reuse in a future task.","Describe how you would explain your ranking to another group."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 4 - Lab - Worksheet | Output Triage Clinic (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w4-lab-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Clinic diagnoses. For each station, record the verdict, a checkable reason, and a verification action.","stem":"Verdict:\nReason:\nVerification action:","lookFor":"Triages the full set with valid, checkable reasons that cite the source or the original text used, and matches a suitable check to each output type rather than using one generic check for all.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w4-lab-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Defend your ladder. Explain why your top output is more trustworthy than your bottom output.","stem":"Top is more trustworthy because ...\nBottom is least trustworthy because ...","lookFor":"Justifies the placement with trustworthiness signals - sources, match to the task, checkability - not preference. Any defensible order with signal-based reasons is acceptable.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w4-lab-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Triage clinic record. Complete one row per station output.","stem":"Station | Verdict | Reason | Verification action | Ladder rank","lookFor":"Every station has a verdict, a valid reason, a chosen verification action and a ladder rank, with the ranking consistent with the reasons recorded.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w4-lab-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: name one risk of using the least trustworthy output as-is, and who it could affect.","lookFor":"States a concrete risk that follows from the specific weakness they found in that output, and names who would be affected if it were used as-is.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w4-lab-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): redesign one unclear output's prompt so its result could be triaged as clearly correct or incorrect.","lookFor":"Re-prompts for specific steps or details, so that the new output would be checkable - an unclear output cannot be judged until it is clarified.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"}]}},"g6-main-w5-core":{"id":"g6-main-w5-core","number":9,"week":5,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Risks and Responsible Use","summary":"Students meet the two basic risk families of AI use - accuracy and safety/privacy - name who each risk could affect, and agree responsible-use rules that reduce them.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["GE"],"objectives":["Identify at least two basic risks of using AI tools (e.g., incorrect output, unsafe or private data use).","Explain why each risk matters and who it could affect.","State one responsible-use rule that reduces a risk."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Risks and Responsible Use","body":["An AI answer can look helpful and still put someone at risk - through a mistake you did not check, or through personal data you should never have shared.","Today you learn to spot those risks and to name the rule that keeps people safe."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can name two genuine risks of using an AI tool.","I can explain who each risk could affect.","I can state a responsible-use rule that reduces a risk."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Risk","definition":"Something that could go wrong when using a tool."},{"term":"Accuracy risk","definition":"The danger of acting on an incorrect output."},{"term":"Privacy","definition":"Keeping personal or identifying information safe and unshared."},{"term":"Personal data","definition":"Information that identifies a person, like a name, address, or photo."},{"term":"Responsible-use rule","definition":"An agreed habit that reduces a risk."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Two risk families","body":["**Accuracy risk** is the danger of using a confident but incorrect output without checking it. The mistake can spread into your work and anyone who trusts it.","**Safety and privacy risk** is the danger of entering [[personal data]] or identifying data, which may be stored or used to train future systems and could expose you or someone else."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Every risk has a who","body":["A real risk always names who could be harmed - you, a classmate, or your family.","[[Responsible-use rule]]s are simple habits, agreed together, that reduce a risk: verify before you use, never enter personal data, and tell an adult if you are unsure."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Sort each scenario into a risk family, name who is affected, and choose a responsible-use rule that would reduce the risk."],"items":["Read the scenario card.","Decide: accuracy risk or safety/privacy risk.","Name who could be affected.","Attach a responsible-use rule that reduces the risk."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which risk do you think is easiest to forget about, and why?","Is a rule useful if it is too hard to follow?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write one responsible-use rule you will follow from now on.","Describe a situation where you would refuse to enter data into a tool."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 5 - Core - Worksheet | Risks and Responsible Use","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w5-core-g6-m-w5-core-q1","type":"shortText","prompt":"For two scenarios, complete the chain: risk, who is affected, responsible-use rule.","stem":"The risk is ...\nIt could affect ...\nA rule that reduces it is ...","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"A genuine, specific risk (not 'it is bad'), a named who - a specific person or group such as the student using the output, a classmate, or their family - and a rule that clearly reduces that named risk. The chain should hold together across both scenarios.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w5-core-g6-m-w5-core-q2","type":"shortText","prompt":"Underline any personal data in a sample prompt and rewrite it safely.","stem":"Personal data found:\nSafe rewrite:","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Identifies identifying details (name, address, photo, ID number) as personal data, and rewrites the prompt with the real name replaced by something like 'a student'. Watch for the misconception that typing in someone's details is fine as long as it helps - personal data may be stored or used to train systems.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w5-core-g6-m-w5-core-q3","type":"shortText","prompt":"Record each risk you find with its who and rule. Complete the table columns: Scenario | Risk family | Who is affected | Responsible-use rule.","stem":"Scenario | Risk family | Who is affected | Responsible-use rule","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Each row sorts the scenario into accuracy or safety/privacy, names a specific who for every risk, and states a matching rule. Emerging: two different, genuine risks with a specific who each and a rule that clearly reduces the named risk.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w5-core-g6-m-w5-core-q4","type":"shortText","prompt":"Name two genuine risks of using AI tools and one responsible-use rule that reduces one of them.","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Two genuinely different risks - e.g. incorrect output used as-is (accuracy) and entering personal data (safety/privacy) - plus one rule that matches one of them, such as 'never enter personal or identifying data into an AI tool'. Redirect vague 'it is bad' answers to a specific risk plus who it affects.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w5-core-g6-m-w5-core-q5","type":"shortText","prompt":"Write a class responsible-use charter of three rules, each matched to a specific risk it reduces.","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Three realistic rules, each explicitly paired with the risk it reduces rather than listed as general advice. Stretch: a scenario that involves both risk families at once, with the overlap explained.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]}]}},"g6-main-w5-lab":{"id":"g6-main-w5-lab","number":10,"week":5,"track":"main","type":"lab","title":"Class AI Risk Audit (Lab)","summary":"Groups act as a class safety board, auditing an anonymised AI use-log against the rules, ranking the gaps on a likelihood-impact matrix, and proposing one rule to fix the top-priority risk.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["GE"],"objectives":["Audit the class's own AI use against a responsible-use rule set and propose one improvement.","Rank risks by likelihood and impact on a simple risk matrix and choose which to mitigate first.","Write one responsible-use rule to close a gap found in the audit."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Class AI Risk Audit (Lab)","body":["Today your group becomes a class safety board. You will audit how AI has actually been used, rank the risks you find, and decide which one to fix first - then write the rule that fixes it."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can audit our AI use against a rule set and find a gap.","I can place risks on a matrix by likelihood and impact.","I can propose one rule to reduce the highest-priority risk."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Audit","definition":"Checking real practice against a set of rules to find gaps."},{"term":"Likelihood","definition":"How often a risk could happen."},{"term":"Impact","definition":"How much harm a risk would cause."},{"term":"Mitigate","definition":"To reduce a risk, often by adding a rule."},{"term":"Gap","definition":"A place where practice does not meet the rule."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Audit against the rules","body":["An [[audit]] compares what actually happened with what should happen. You read the anonymised class use-log and flag any entry that breaks or stretches a responsible-use rule.","The log is anonymised on purpose: you fix systems and habits, *never* blame a person."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Rank, then rule","body":["Place each flagged risk on the matrix by its [[likelihood]] and [[impact]], then pick the highest-priority one.","Finally, write one realistic responsible-use rule that would close that gap - and be ready to pitch it for the class charter."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Audit the anonymised use-log, flag the gaps, plot the risks on the matrix, and propose one rule to fix the top-priority risk."],"items":["Read each log entry against the rule set.","Flag gaps and name the risk and who it affects.","Plot risks on the likelihood-impact matrix.","Circle the highest-priority risk.","Write and pitch one mitigation rule."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which risk surprised you as higher priority than expected?","How do you make a rule strong but still realistic?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write one change you will make to your own AI use after this audit.","Explain why anonymising the log matters."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 5 - Lab - Worksheet | Class AI Risk Audit (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w5-lab-g6-m-w5-lab-q1","type":"shortText","prompt":"For each log entry, note whether it meets the rules and flag any gap.","stem":"Meets rules? Yes / No\nGap found:\nRisk and who:","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Finds a genuine gap - e.g. a demo prompt that included a student's name - and names both the risk (privacy exposure) and the who (that student). Watch for blame-focused answers: the log is anonymised, and audits fix systems, not people.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w5-lab-g6-m-w5-lab-q2","type":"shortText","prompt":"Explain why your top risk is highest priority and write a rule to fix it.","stem":"Top risk because ...\nMitigation rule:","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Priority justified by matrix position - the high-likelihood, high-impact risk (usually privacy) comes first, rather than 'fix everything at once'. The rule must be realistic and actually reduce the named risk, e.g. replace any real name with 'a student' before using a prompt.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w5-lab-g6-m-w5-lab-q3","type":"shortText","prompt":"Record each flagged risk with its matrix placement and priority. Complete the table columns: Log entry | Gap / risk | Likelihood | Impact | Priority.","stem":"Log entry | Gap / risk | Likelihood | Impact | Priority","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Risks placed sensibly on likelihood and impact with a justified priority order, each row tied back to a real log entry. Approaching: risks placed but priority weakly justified.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w5-lab-g6-m-w5-lab-q4","type":"shortText","prompt":"State the highest-priority risk your group found and the one rule you would add to reduce it.","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Names one genuine top-priority risk consistent with the group's matrix, plus one workable rule that reduces it. Emerging: a realistic rule that reduces the top risk.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w5-lab-g6-m-w5-lab-q5","type":"shortText","prompt":"Design a one-page class responsible-use charter that closes every gap your audit found, ordered by priority.","autoMarked":false,"lookFor":"Every flagged gap is covered by a rule, and the order follows the matrix. Stretch: justifies a near-tie placement and explains how a rule would move a risk down the matrix.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]}]}},"g6-main-w6-core":{"id":"g6-main-w6-core","number":11,"week":6,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Bringing the Strands Together","summary":"Students run one guided challenge through all four strands and explain how each link in the chain shaped the next.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Complete a guided challenge combining the concept, a prompted solution, an output check and a risk check.","Explain how the system type and prompt shaped the output.","Identify one responsible-use choice made."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["This term you learned four skills one at a time. Today you discover they were never separate - they form one chain, from understanding the system to writing a prompt, checking the output, and using it responsibly. Put all four hats on at once."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can complete a guided challenge that uses all four strands.","I can explain how the system type and prompt shaped the output.","I can name one responsible-use choice I made."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Integration","definition":"Connecting separate skills into one process."},{"term":"System type","definition":"Whether a system is rule-based or learning-based."},{"term":"Prompt","definition":"The instruction you give an AI tool to guide its output."},{"term":"Triage","definition":"Judging an output as correct, incorrect, or unclear."},{"term":"Risk check","definition":"Asking what could go wrong and choosing a responsible action."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"One connected chain","body":["The [[system type]] shapes what you can expect. The [[prompt]] shapes the output. The output needs a [[triage]]. And the whole process needs a [[risk check]].","Each strand feeds the next - miss one link and the chain weakens."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Explain the links","body":["It is not enough to complete each step; you should be able to explain how one shaped the next.","For example: because the prompt was specific, the output stayed on task, which made triage easier and the risk lower."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Run one guided challenge through all four strands, then explain how the system and prompt shaped the output and name one responsible-use choice you made."],"items":["Name the system type behind the task.","Write or choose a clear prompt.","Triage the curated output.","Run a basic risk check and note a responsible-use choice.","Explain how the system and prompt shaped the output."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which strand was strongest in your work, and which needs practice?","How would a weaker prompt have changed the whole chain?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write which strand you will focus on before the signature solution.","Explain one link in the chain in your own words."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 6 - Core - Worksheet | Bringing the Strands Together","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w6-core-q1","type":"shortText","prompt":"Complete each of the four steps for the guided challenge.","stem":"System type:\nMy prompt:\nTriage verdict and reason:\nRisk check and choice:","lookFor":"All four steps recorded: a named system type (e.g. a learning system for a text summariser, contrasted with rule-based), a specific prompt, a triage verdict of correct / incorrect / unclear with a checkable reason, and a genuine risk-reduction choice such as keeping personal data out of the prompt.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w6-core-q2","type":"shortText","prompt":"Write two sentences linking system, prompt, and output.","stem":"Because the system was ...\nThe prompt made the output ... which means ...","lookFor":"Both stems completed so that system and prompt are clearly linked to the output - e.g. a specific prompt gave a focused, on-task summary while a vague one would drift. Approaching links only two strands; not yet treats the strands as separate.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w6-core-q3","type":"shortText","prompt":"Record your result for each strand.","stem":"Strand | What I did | Result | Link to next strand","lookFor":"One row per strand with the action taken, a result, and a link showing how each strand fed the next: system type shapes the prompt, the prompt shapes the output, the output demands a triage, and the whole process demands a responsible-use choice.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w6-core-q4","type":"shortText","prompt":"Explain how the system type and prompt shaped your output, and name one responsible-use choice you made.","stem":"","lookFor":"Connects system plus prompt to the output quality and names a genuine risk-reduction choice that was made during the task (e.g. no personal data in the prompt), not added afterwards.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w6-core-q5","type":"shortText","prompt":"Redesign the prompt to lower the risk further, and predict how the output and triage would change.","stem":"","lookFor":"Redesigned prompt targets the risk that was identified (e.g. removes personal data, asks for checkable statements) and the prediction explains how both the output and the risk would change - the stretch expectation for this lesson.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]}]}},"g6-main-w6-lab":{"id":"g6-main-w6-lab","number":12,"week":6,"track":"main","type":"lab","title":"Integrated Build-and-Check (Lab)","summary":"Students run a full build-and-check task across four stations, record accuracy and risk verdicts, decide whether to use the output, and diagnose their weakest strand.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Run a small integrated build-and-check task end-to-end and record the checks made.","Identify which strand most needs work before the signature solution.","Evaluate the final output for accuracy and risk before deciding to use it."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["This is your dress rehearsal. You will run a real task from start to finish - build it with a system and a prompt, then check it for accuracy and risk before deciding whether to use it. By the end you will know exactly which strand to sharpen before the signature solution."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can run a build-and-check task through all four strands end-to-end.","I can record the accuracy and risk checks I made.","I can name which strand I most need to improve."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Build-and-check","definition":"Producing an AI solution and then testing it before use."},{"term":"Accuracy verdict","definition":"Whether the output is correct, incorrect, or unclear."},{"term":"Risk verdict","definition":"How risky the output is and what would reduce it."},{"term":"Mitigation","definition":"An action that reduces a risk."},{"term":"Weakest link","definition":"The strand most likely to fail under pressure."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Build, then check","body":["Building means choosing a suitable system and designing a clear [[prompt]] to reach a useful output.","Checking means triaging that output for accuracy and running a risk assessment before you decide to use it. Building without checking just produces confident mistakes."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Decide and diagnose","body":["Your final call is **use**, **do not use**, or **use-with-changes** - and you must justify it with your two verdicts.","Then diagnose your [[weakest link]]: the strand where your evidence was thinnest. That is your focus before the signature solution."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Run the four stations end-to-end. Build with a system and prompt, check for accuracy and risk, decide whether to use the output, and diagnose your weakest strand."],"items":["Name the system type and why it suits the task.","Design a clear, specific prompt.","Triage the curated output for accuracy.","Run a risk assessment and choose a mitigation.","Decide use / do not use / use-with-changes and diagnose your weakest strand."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Did your accuracy verdict or your risk verdict decide your use decision?","Which strand was your weakest link, and how will you strengthen it?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write your focus strand and one action to improve it before the signature solution.","Explain why building without checking is risky."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 6 - Lab - Worksheet | Integrated Build-and-Check (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w6-lab-q1","type":"shortText","prompt":"Record your evidence at each of the four stations.","stem":"System type + why:\nPrompt:\nAccuracy verdict + reason:\nRisk verdict + mitigation:","lookFor":"System choice justified by the task (e.g. a learning system that finds language patterns, suited because summarising needs pattern recognition rather than fixed rules), a specific prompt, an accuracy verdict with a reason that can be checked against the source, and a risk verdict whose mitigation matches the risk (e.g. main risk is using an unverified detail; mitigation is verify before use).","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w6-lab-q2","type":"shortText","prompt":"Make your use decision and name your weakest strand with a reason.","stem":"Decision: use / do not use / use-with-changes because ...\nWeakest strand:\nAction to improve:","lookFor":"A justified decision that treats use-with-changes as a real third option rather than a yes/no, plus an honest and specific weakest-strand diagnosis justified by where the evidence was thinnest, with one concrete improvement action.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w6-lab-q3","type":"shortText","prompt":"Complete one row per station and finish with your decision.","stem":"Station / strand | Evidence produced | Verdict | Note for signature solution","lookFor":"All four stations completed with recorded evidence and a verdict for each, finishing with the use decision. Emerging completes all four strands with recorded evidence; approaching completes most stations with some evidence.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w6-lab-q4","type":"shortText","prompt":"State your use decision with a reason, and name the strand you most need to improve before the signature solution.","stem":"","lookFor":"The decision is tied back to the accuracy and risk verdicts rather than to how the output looks, and the named strand is specific enough to act on before the signature solution.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w6-lab-q5","type":"shortText","prompt":"Rewrite your prompt so the output would earn a lower risk verdict, and predict how the use decision would change.","stem":"","lookFor":"Rewritten prompt visibly reduces the risk named at station 4 and the prediction explains the shift in the use decision (e.g. from use-with-changes to use) - the stretch expectation, which may also include a written justification of the use-with-changes option.","autoMarked":false,"frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]}]}},"g6-main-w7-core":{"id":"g6-main-w7-core","number":13,"week":7,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Signature Solution - Planning","summary":"Students plan a five-part signature solution - goal, prompt structure, inputs, evaluation method and risk plan - and take it through an approve-to-build gate.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Plan a structured AI solution: state the goal, the prompt structure, and the check steps.","Identify the inputs/data and the output-evaluation method.","Describe one risk and how the plan reduces it."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Today you become a designer, not just a prompter. Before anyone types a single word into an AI tool, you will plan a small solution with five parts that fit together: a goal, a prompt structure, inputs, a check, and a risk plan. A good plan is like a bridge design drawn before the first bolt is placed - it lets you find weak spots on paper, where fixing them is cheap."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can state the goal of my solution in one clear sentence.","I can lay out a prompt structure with a role, a task, and one rule.","I can name the inputs my solution needs and how I will check the output.","I can describe one risk and one way my plan reduces it."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Signature solution","definition":"A small, reusable AI design with a clear goal and connected parts that solves one simple task."},{"term":"Prompt structure","definition":"The shape of a prompt: a role for the AI, a clear task, and at least one rule or limit."},{"term":"Input","definition":"The information a prompt works on, chosen to contain no personal or identifying data."},{"term":"Evaluation method","definition":"The agreed way you will judge whether an output is accurate."},{"term":"Risk mitigation","definition":"A design choice that lowers the chance or impact of something going wrong."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"From one prompt to a whole solution","body":["In Grade 5 you improved a single prompt. This term you design a solution: a set of parts that work together to do one simple job well.","The five parts are the goal, the [[prompt structure]], the inputs, the [[evaluation method]], and the risk plan. If any part is missing, the solution is harder to trust."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Writing a prompt structure","body":["A [[prompt structure]] names a role ('You are a timetable helper'), a task ('list the three earliest buses'), and one rule ('if a time is missing, say so').","The role focuses the AI, the task says exactly what to do, and the rule keeps the output honest and easy to check."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Checks and risks before you build","body":["An [[evaluation method]] says how you will judge accuracy, such as comparing the output to a known-correct answer key.","A risk plan names one thing that could go wrong, like a confident wrong answer, and one design choice that reduces it, like a 'say if unsure' rule plus a human check. Never put personal or identifying data into a prompt."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will now plan your own signature solution on the plan sheet. You will not log in to any tool; the teacher runs any AI on the board. Your job is to make the plan complete and safe."],"items":["Read your task card and write the goal in one clear sentence.","Fill the role-task-rule slots for your prompt structure.","List the inputs your prompt needs, checking there is no personal data.","Write your evaluation method and one risk with its mitigation.","Self-check against the five-part list, then submit for the approve-to-build gate."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which part of a plan is easiest to forget, and why does that matter?","How does a 'say if unsure' rule make an output safer to trust?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the one part of your plan you are most confident about.","Write the one part you will strengthen before building next week."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 7 - Core - Worksheet | Signature Solution - Planning","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w7-core-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Name the five parts: using your task card, complete each part of the plan in one line.","stem":"The goal is to ___.\nThe AI acts as a ___ (role).\nThe task is to ___.\nOne rule is ___.\nThe inputs are ___ (no personal data).\nI will check accuracy by ___.\nOne risk is ___; my plan reduces it by ___.","lookFor":"All five parts are present: a goal naming the task and who it helps, a role + task + one rule structure, inputs with no personal or identifying data, a checkable evaluation method (e.g. compare labelled items to a teacher answer key), and one risk with a mitigation that clearly reduces that named risk. Evaluation and risk are the parts most often left blank or thin.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w7-core-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Spot the weak plan: read the two sample plans provided","stem":"The stronger plan is ___ because ___.","lookFor":"Chooses the complete plan and names a specific missing part - the evaluation method or the risk row - as the reason, rather than saying the plan is longer or neater. Structure beats size: a missing check is a hidden weak spot.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w7-core-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"My plan review record: fill one row for each of the five parts (plan part / what I wrote / ready? Y/N) and mark whether it is ready.","lookFor":"One row per part, each quoting what the student actually wrote plus an honest Y/N. Check the evaluation and risk rows hardest: a 'Y' there should sit beside a method that can really be checked and a mitigation tied to the named risk.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w7-core-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: write your goal sentence and the one risk your plan reduces.","lookFor":"Goal is one clear sentence saying what the solution does and who it helps. The risk is realistic (e.g. a confident but wrong answer) and is one the plan genuinely reduces through its rule, check or human step.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w7-core-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): add a second rule to your prompt structure and explain which kind of wrong answer it is designed to stop.","lookFor":"Writes a second rule that fits the same prompt structure and names the kind of wrong answer it blocks (e.g. a confident guess, a dropped item, an unclear label). The link between the rule and the error it stops is what to look for.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"}]}},"g6-main-w7-lab":{"id":"g6-main-w7-lab","number":14,"week":7,"track":"main","type":"lab","title":"Prototype and Pressure-Test the Plan (Lab)","summary":"Students prototype their approved plan on the board, break it on purpose with a tricky curated input, and design an if-then fallback branch that keeps a person in the loop.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Prototype the prompt structure and pressure-test the plan against a tricky input.","Add a fallback branch for when the output fails a check.","Record what changed and why after the pressure test."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["A plan on paper looks tidy. Today you find out how it behaves under pressure. You will build a quick prototype, then throw your hardest inputs at it on purpose - the missing item, the messy note, the two-in-one line. When it stumbles, you will not hide the failure; you will design a fallback branch that catches it safely. Engineers stress-test bridges before people cross them. You will stress-test your solution before it is trusted."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can run my prompt structure on a normal input and read the output.","I can pressure-test it with a tricky input and spot where it fails.","I can add a fallback branch that says what to do when a check fails.","I can record the change I made and the reason for it."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Prototype","definition":"A first working version built quickly to see how a design really behaves."},{"term":"Pressure test","definition":"Deliberately trying a tricky input to reveal a weak spot."},{"term":"Edge case","definition":"An unusual input at the edge of what a solution expects, like a missing or double value."},{"term":"Fallback branch","definition":"A planned 'if a check fails, then...' response that handles a failure safely."},{"term":"Error type","definition":"A label for what went wrong: wrong, unclear, or missing."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Prototype first, judge second","body":["A [[prototype]] is a fast first version. Running it shows how your design behaves, not just how you hoped it would.","The teacher runs your prompt on the board with a normal curated input. You read the output and decide: did it meet the goal?"]},{"type":"text","heading":"Design the fallback branch","body":["When a check fails, a good solution follows a [[fallback branch]] instead of giving a confident wrong answer.","Write it as 'If the check fails, then ___ and ask a person.' Keeping a human in the loop is part of responsible design.","Remember: only curated, made-up inputs - never real names or personal data."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will prototype your approved plan, pressure-test it, and add a fallback branch. The teacher runs the AI on the board; you do not log in. Log every run."],"items":["Copy your prompt structure into the prototype slot of your log.","Watch the normal-input run on the board and record whether it met the goal.","Pressure-test with a tricky curated input and record the error type.","Write your fallback branch as an if-then sentence that asks a person.","Log the one change you made and the reason it will help."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which tricky input broke the most prototypes, and why?","Why is a handled failure safer than a confident wrong answer?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the weak spot your pressure test exposed.","Write how your fallback branch will handle it next week."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 7 - Lab - Worksheet | Prototype and Pressure-Test the Plan (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w7-lab-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Predict then test: before the board run, predict how your prompt will handle one tricky input. After the run, record what actually happened.","stem":"I predict the output will be ___.\nThe actual output was ___.\nThe error type was ___ (wrong / unclear / missing).","lookFor":"The prediction is written before the run and the actual output after it, and the error type is one of the three labels. A real pressure test exposes something - e.g. 'found: charger and a scarf' returning 'devices' only is a missing error, not a wrong one.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w7-lab-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Write your fallback branch: turn your exposed failure into a safe fallback using the if-then frame","stem":"If ___ then ___ and ask a person.","lookFor":"The branch handles the failure the pressure test actually exposed and keeps a person in the loop (e.g. 'if a notice names two items, then label each one separately, and if still unclear label it other and ask a person'). A vague branch, or one that does not match the observed failure, is only approaching.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w7-lab-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Prototype build/test log: record each run - the input used (curated), the output, whether it met the goal (Y/N), and the error type if any.","lookFor":"At least one normal run and one pressure test are logged, each with its curated input, the output, a Y/N against the goal, and a classified error type for any run that failed. Logs without an error type are returned.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w7-lab-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: write the weak spot you found and the fallback branch you designed for it.","lookFor":"Names one specific weak spot from their own log, not a general worry, and gives a fallback branch that visibly answers that weak spot.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"},{"id":"g6-main-w7-lab-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): design a second fallback for a different error type and say which failure is more likely and why.","lookFor":"A second branch aimed at a different error type (wrong / unclear / missing) from the first, plus a judgement about which failure is more likely with a reason drawn from the runs they logged.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"tierProbed":"emerging","evidenceType":"product"}]}},"g6-main-w8-core":{"id":"g6-main-w8-core","number":15,"week":8,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Signature Solution - Building","summary":"Students build their signature solution by following their approved plan, evaluate and flag every output, and log one evidence-based improvement as evaluate -> change -> result.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Build the solution by following the plan and prompt structure.","Evaluate outputs for accuracy and flag any that are incorrect or unclear.","Make at least one improvement based on an output check.","Keep personal and identifying data out of every prompt and keep a person in the loop for anything flagged."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["Today you build for real. You will follow your approved plan, run your inputs, and look hard at every output - not just the ones that look right. When you find an answer that is wrong or unclear, you flag it, change one thing, and watch what happens. This is how real systems get better: one careful change, one honest result. Keep every prompt free of personal data, and keep a person in the loop for anything you flag."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can build my solution by following my approved plan.","I can evaluate outputs and flag ones that are wrong or unclear.","I can make one improvement and explain what changed and why.","I can keep personal data out of every prompt."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Build","definition":"Turning your approved plan into a working solution by following it step by step."},{"term":"Evaluate","definition":"Judging an output against your check to decide if it is correct and clear."},{"term":"Flag","definition":"Marking an output that is wrong or unclear so it gets attention and a fix."},{"term":"Improvement","definition":"One deliberate change made in response to an evaluation."},{"term":"Iteration loop","definition":"The evaluate -> change -> result cycle that makes a solution better over time."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Follow the plan, do not improvise","body":["Building means running your approved prompt structure on defined inputs, not inventing new prompts on the spot.","Record each output before you change anything. That way you can see what your solution really does."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Evaluate, flag, improve","body":["Evaluate each output against your check: correct, unclear, or wrong. Flag anything that fails.","Then make one improvement and re-run. Record it as **evaluate -> change -> result** so the cause of the improvement is clear."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will build your solution from your approved plan, evaluate the outputs, flag errors, and log one improvement. The teacher runs the AI on the board; you do not log in."],"items":["Restate your goal and check on your log.","Run your prompt structure on defined curated inputs and record each output.","Evaluate each output and flag the wrong or unclear ones.","Make one improvement in response to a flag and re-run it.","Log the loop as evaluate -> change -> result."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["How did you tell a subtly wrong output from a correct one?","Why is one change at a time better than many at once?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the one improvement you made and why.","Write what you would evaluate more carefully next time."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 8 - Core - Worksheet | Signature Solution - Building","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w8-core-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Flag the errors: read the five curated outputs provided. Mark each correct, unclear, or wrong and flag the ones that fail the check.","stem":"Output ___ is ___ (correct / unclear / wrong) because ___.","lookFor":"Judges each output against the known-correct reference rather than a hunch, catches both seeded errors among the correct-looking rows, and gives a reason tied to the check (for example, 'other' for a tablet charger is wrong because it should be 'devices').","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w8-core-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Log one improvement: choose one flagged output and record your improvement using the loop frame.","stem":"Evaluate: ___.\nChange: ___.\nResult: ___.","lookFor":"One change only, made in response to a specific flag, with the observed result recorded - for example 'Evaluate: charger mislabelled. Change: added a devices examples rule. Result: charger now devices, accuracy up.' Cause and effect should be visible in the loop.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w8-core-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Build and improvement log: record each output, your judgement, whether you flagged it, and the improvement you made. Use the columns Input (curated), Output, Judgement, Improvement made.","lookFor":"The build follows the approved plan and prompt structure with defined, curated inputs rather than improvised prompts; every row is evaluated and flagged rather than only the obvious ones; all inputs are free of personal or identifying data.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w8-core-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: write your one improvement as evaluate -> change -> result.","lookFor":"All three parts of the loop are present and connected, showing the change was driven by an evaluation and that the result was actually observed, not assumed.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w8-core-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): make a second improvement and use your log to say which change raised accuracy more, with evidence.","lookFor":"Compares the two changes using evidence from the log rather than impression, and recognises that one change at a time is what makes the comparison possible.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"}]}},"g6-main-w8-lab":{"id":"g6-main-w8-lab","number":16,"week":8,"track":"main","type":"lab","title":"Extended Build and Evaluation (Lab)","summary":"Students complete their solution, run it across a six-input curated test set to report an accuracy count, handle a failure gracefully with their fallback, and finish with a three-question risk check.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Complete a working solution and evaluate its outputs across several test inputs.","Handle an incorrect output gracefully and document the fix.","Run a basic risk check and confirm no personal data is used."],"sections":[{"type":"text","body":["This is the finish line for your signature solution - but finishing is not just getting one right answer. Today you prove it works by running it across several test inputs and counting how many it gets right. When one fails, your fallback catches it gracefully and you document the fix. Finally you run a basic risk check: is it accurate, is it clear, and is any prompt free of personal data? A solution you can measure and defend is one people can trust."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can complete a working solution that follows my plan.","I can evaluate its outputs across several test inputs and measure accuracy.","I can handle an incorrect output gracefully using my fallback.","I can run a basic risk check and document the fix I made."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Test set","definition":"A small, curated group of inputs, including edge cases, used to measure how a solution performs."},{"term":"Accuracy count","definition":"The number of correct outputs out of the total tested, such as 5 out of 6."},{"term":"Graceful handling","definition":"Using the fallback so an incorrect output is caught safely, not presented as final."},{"term":"Documented fix","definition":"A written record of the change made to correct a failure and its effect."},{"term":"Risk check","definition":"A quick review of accuracy, clarity, and data safety before a solution is trusted."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Prove it across several inputs","body":["One correct answer is not proof. Run your finished solution across a **test set** of several curated inputs and count how many outputs are correct.","Report accuracy as a simple count, like 5 out of 6, so your claim is honest and checkable."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Handle failures and check the risks","body":["When an output is wrong, let your fallback catch it and keep a person in the loop, then document the fix and its effect.","Finish with a basic risk check: accurate enough? clear enough? any personal data used? The data answer must always be no, because we use only curated inputs."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["You will complete your solution, evaluate it across six curated inputs, handle any failure with your fallback, document the fix, and run a basic risk check. The teacher runs the AI on the board; you do not log in."],"items":["Confirm your build follows the plan and the fallback is wired in.","Define what counts as a correct output.","Run all six curated inputs and record each result.","Compute accuracy and handle any failure with your fallback, documenting the fix.","Complete the three-question risk check and note the biggest remaining risk."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Why is an accuracy count across several inputs fairer than judging one output?","What is the biggest remaining risk in your solution, and who should check it?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write your accuracy result and the fix you documented.","Write one thing your risk check made you notice."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 8 - Lab - Worksheet | Extended Build and Evaluation (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w8-lab-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Run and count: record the result of each of the six curated test inputs, mark correct or not, and compute your accuracy count.","stem":"Input ___ output ___ is ___ (correct / not).\nMy accuracy is ___ out of 6.","lookFor":"Accuracy is stated as a count across the whole test set (for example 5 out of 6), judged against a definition of a correct output agreed before testing - not a claim based on one lucky case.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w8-lab-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Document the fix and risk check: record how your fallback handled a failure, the fix you made, and your three-question risk check.","stem":"The fallback handled ___ by ___.\nMy fix was ___ and the result was ___.\nRisk check - accurate? ___ clear? ___ personal data used? no.","lookFor":"Graceful handling means the fallback caught the failure and kept a person in the loop rather than the wrong output being deleted or presented as final; the fix is traceable with its effect recorded; all three risk questions are answered and the personal-data answer is no because every input is curated.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w8-lab-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Test-set evaluation and risk record: fill one row per test input with its result, then complete the risk-check summary below. Use the columns Input (curated), Output, Correct? (Y/N), Note / fix.","lookFor":"A completed solution that follows the plan with the fallback wired in, evaluated across all six inputs with an accuracy count, and a risk-check summary covering accuracy, clarity and data safety.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w8-lab-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: write your accuracy count and confirm your risk check found no personal data.","lookFor":"States accuracy as correct out of total and confirms explicitly that no personal or identifying data was used, linking that to the inputs being curated.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"},{"id":"g6-main-w8-lab-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): add a seventh harder edge case, predict the result, test it, and say whether your solution generalised and why.","lookFor":"Makes a prediction before testing, compares it with the actual result, and reasons about whether the solution handles inputs it has not seen - naming rare unseen edge cases as a remaining risk.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}],"evidenceType":"product","tierProbed":"emerging"}]}},"g6-main-w9-core":{"id":"g6-main-w9-core","number":17,"week":9,"track":"main","type":"core","title":"Signature Solution - Refining & Finishing","summary":"Students judge their signature solution against accuracy, usefulness and risk, then make one improvement and prove with re-testing that it helped.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Finish the solution so it meets the agreed success criteria.","Evaluate it for accuracy, usefulness and risk.","Explain one change that improved it."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Overview","body":["You are almost there. This week you become the judge of your own work, checking it for accuracy, usefulness and risk, then making one smart change that you can prove made it better."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can finish my solution so it meets the success criteria our class agreed on.","I can judge my solution for accuracy, usefulness and risk using the rubric.","I can explain one change I made and say why it made the solution better."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Refine","definition":"To improve something on purpose by comparing it to your goals."},{"term":"Accuracy","definition":"How often the system gives the right or sensible answer."},{"term":"Usefulness","definition":"How much the solution really helps the user do their task."},{"term":"Risk","definition":"What could go wrong or who could be harmed, including privacy."},{"term":"Evidence","definition":"Proof from testing, not just an opinion."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Judging your solution three ways","body":["A good builder does not just ask 'do I like it?'. You measure your work against the criteria the class agreed.","**Accuracy**: test with examples where you know the right answer. **Usefulness**: check it helps your real user. **Risk**: ask what could go wrong and whether any private data is exposed."]},{"type":"text","heading":"One change, proven","body":["You do not need many changes. Choose the one improvement that matters most to your user.","Make the change, then re-test. If accuracy went up and nothing else broke, you have evidence it worked. Write it in your refinement note."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["Work with your partner. Judge your solution against the rubric, choose one improvement, make it, and prove it helped."],"items":["Read the three lenses on the criteria card.","Run one accuracy test with known answers and record the score.","Check usefulness against your real user's task.","Do a basic risk check for harm, fairness and privacy.","Pick one improvement, make it, and re-test.","Swap for 'two stars and a wish' feedback."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which lens was hardest to judge, and why?","How did re-testing change your confidence in your solution?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write the one change you made and the evidence it helped.","Name one risk you are now sure your solution avoids."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 9 - Core - Worksheet | Signature Solution - Refining & Finishing","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w9-core-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Three-lens evaluation: judge your solution on each lens. Give a score or a yes/no and the evidence behind it.","stem":"Accuracy: I tested ___ and got ___\nUsefulness: it helps my user because ___\nRisk: I checked ___ so ___","lookFor":"Judges all three lenses with evidence from testing: known-answer test cases rather than opinion for accuracy, a usefulness judgement linked to the real user's task, and a harm, fairness or privacy risk that was actually checked.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w9-core-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"One improvement, proven: describe the single change you made and show before and after evidence.","stem":"The change I made was ___\nBefore: ___ After: ___\nNothing else broke because ___","lookFor":"One targeted change (a fixed or added rule, or cleaner example data) with before/after evidence, and a re-test confirming it helped without breaking another case.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w9-core-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Refinement log: record each check, your judgement and the evidence.","stem":"Lens | Judgement | Evidence | Action taken","lookFor":"All three lenses logged, each with a judgement, the evidence it came from, and the action taken; evidence traces back to testing rather than opinion.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w9-core-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: what one change improved your solution most, and how do you know?","lookFor":"Meets the Emerging criteria: names an accuracy, usefulness or risk judgement and one improvement.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w9-core-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): compare two possible improvements and justify, with evidence, why you chose one over the other.","lookFor":"Justifies why the chosen improvement beats a second candidate improvement, using evidence.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]}]}},"g6-main-w9-lab":{"id":"g6-main-w9-lab","number":18,"week":9,"track":"main","type":"lab","title":"Final Iteration and Risk Review (Lab)","summary":"Students make their final improvement, sort risks on a likelihood-by-seriousness matrix, confirm the solution is safe and fair, and freeze the evaluated version for showcase.","duration":"45 minutes","strands":["CU","SD","CE","GE"],"objectives":["Run a final evaluation across accuracy, usefulness and risk, and act on one improvement.","Complete a risk review and confirm the solution is safe and fair to use.","Document the final iteration with before-and-after evidence."],"sections":[{"type":"text","heading":"Overview","body":["Time for the final polish. In this Lab you make your last improvement, then act like a safety checker, running a full risk review so you can promise your solution is safe and fair before showcase."]},{"type":"list","heading":"I can…","items":["I can run a final evaluation across accuracy, usefulness and risk and act on one improvement.","I can complete a risk review and confirm my solution is safe and fair.","I can record my final iteration with clear before-and-after evidence."]},{"type":"vocabulary","heading":"Words to know","terms":[{"term":"Iteration","definition":"One more round of improving and re-testing."},{"term":"Likelihood","definition":"How likely a risk is to actually happen."},{"term":"Seriousness","definition":"How much harm a risk would cause if it happened."},{"term":"Risk matrix","definition":"A grid that sorts risks by likelihood and seriousness."},{"term":"Freeze","definition":"To stop changing the version you have evaluated."}]},{"type":"text","heading":"Sorting risks the smart way","body":["Not every risk is equal. A risk that is both likely and serious needs action now. A rare but very serious risk still needs a plan.","Place each risk on the [[risk matrix]], then decide: fix it now, or watch it. Always confirm no personal data is anywhere in your solution."]},{"type":"text","heading":"Finish, then freeze","body":["Make your final planned change and re-test with known answers. Record before and after so others can trust your evidence.","Then **freeze** the version. The solution you show at the carousel must be the exact one you tested and judged safe."]},{"type":"activity","label":"Let's try it - hands-on","heading":"Let's try it - hands-on","body":["With your partner, make the final change, re-test, run the full risk review, and freeze your solution ready for showcase."],"items":["Set your Lab goal from the refinement log.","Make the final planned improvement.","Re-test with at least five known-answer cases and record before and after.","List risks and place each on the matrix.","Decide actions and confirm no personal data is used.","Write a safe-and-fair statement and freeze the version."]},{"type":"list","heading":"Think about it","items":["Which risk surprised you most when you placed it on the matrix?","How did freezing change how carefully you tested?"]},{"type":"list","heading":"My reflection","items":["Write your safe-and-fair statement in one sentence.","Name the risk you are most confident you have managed."]}],"worksheet":{"title":"Week 9 - Lab - Worksheet | Final Iteration and Risk Review (Lab)","questions":[{"id":"g6-main-w9-lab-q1","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Risk matrix: list three risks and place each by likelihood and seriousness, then decide an action.","stem":"Risk 1: ___ likelihood ___ seriousness ___ action ___\nRisk 2: ___\nRisk 3: ___","lookFor":"Places risks by likelihood and seriousness and decides actions; look for both likelihood and seriousness reasoning, and note that a rare risk can still be serious enough to need a plan.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w9-lab-q2","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Final iteration evidence: record your last change and the before-and-after test results.","stem":"Final change: ___\nBefore: ___ After: ___\nSafe-and-fair statement: ___","lookFor":"The final change is shown with before/after numbers from re-testing, and the safe-and-fair statement covers both privacy and fairness.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w9-lab-q3","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Final iteration log: log each risk, its matrix position, the action, and confirm privacy.","stem":"Risk | Likelihood | Seriousness | Action / privacy check","lookFor":"Each risk carries a matrix position and an action, and the log confirms no personal data is used anywhere in the solution.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w9-lab-q4","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Exit ticket: write your safe-and-fair statement and one risk you managed with evidence.","lookFor":"Meets the Emerging criteria: confirms no personal data is used and that the solution is fair, and names one risk acted on with evidence.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]},{"id":"g6-main-w9-lab-q5","type":"shortText","autoMarked":false,"prompt":"Challenge (stretch): redesign one part of your solution so a high risk moves to a lower box on the matrix, and justify the change.","lookFor":"Proposes a design change that would move a risk to a lower box on the matrix, and justifies it.","frameworkRefs":[{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CU","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CU·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"SD","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·SD·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"CE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·CE·G6·E"},{"framework":"AIF","strand":"GE","grade":6,"tier":"E","code":"AIF·GE·G6·E"}]}]}}}}};

